	\begin{abstract} Different attribution-scores have been proposed to quantify the relevance of database tuples for a query answer from a database. Among them, we find Causal Responsibility, the Shapley Value, the Banzhaf Power-Index, and the Causal Effect. They have been analyzed in isolation, mainly  in terms of computational properties. In this work, we start an investigation into the alignment of these scores on the basis of the queries at hand; that is, on whether they induce compatible rankings of tuples. We are able to identify vast classes of queries for which some pairs of scores are always aligned, and others for which they are not. It turns out that the presence of exogenous tuples makes a crucial difference in this regard.

   % \keywords{Causality, Probabilistic Database, Responsibility, Shapley Value}
	\end{abstract}





	\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

    Explanations for query answers in databases (DBs) can be provided in terms of {\em attribution scores}. Assigned as numbers to DB tuples, they quantify the relevance or importance of a tuple to a query answer at hand.
    \ Among those scores, we find {\em Causal Responsibility} \cite{QA_Causality,tocs}, {\em Resilience}  \cite{wolfgang} (closely related to the former), the {\em Shapley Value} \cite{Shapley_Tuple_Bertossi,sigRec21,sigRec23}), the {\em Banzhaf Power-Index} \cite{Shapley_Tuple_Bertossi} (related to the former), and the  {\em Causal Effect} \cite{Causal_Effect}.
     \
    These are all {\em local scores}, i.e. for a single query answer obtained from a relational DB \cite{bda22}.  There have been extensions of the Shapley value and the Causal Effect to probabilistic databases (PDBs) in \cite{senellart24} and \cite{clear25}, resp.

    The above mentioned scores have been investigated in isolation from each other, in terms of their computation, complexity, and general properties. However, comparisons among them in terms of the results they return has received little attention. There is not reason to expect that those attribution scores give us the same {\em numbers}. However, we might be more interested in knowing whether they {\em rank} the database tuples in the same order. This is important in that we may be more interested in knowing  which are the most relevant tuples for the query answer rather than in their specific numeric scores. \ If this is the case, and we know in advance that, for a certain class of queries, the scores are going to be {\em aligned} in that they induce the same rankings of tuples, for any DB instance, we can arbitrarily use any of them.
 This is the problem we investigate in this work.

 We concentrate on three of those scores when applied to DBs: Responsibility, Shapley Value, and Causal Effect, denoted with {\sf Resp}, {\sf Shapley} and {\sf CES}, resp. We unveil conditions on queries and DBs that lead to score alignment or the lack of it. Although some of these scores have been extended to PDBs \cite{senellart,clear25},  we consider only non-probabilistic relational DBs.

    {\sf Resp} is based on {\em responsibility} \cite{chockler} as introduced in the context of {\em actual causality} and  {\em counterfactual interventions} \cite{HP05,halpern15,H16}. The latter can be seen as hypothetical changes performed on a causal model, to identify other changes. By doing so, one can detect cause-effect relationships.


    The Shapley Value \cite{shapley1953original,roth1988shapley} is well-known in  \emph{Coalition Game Theory} as a measure of the contribution of individual players to a shared {\em game (or wealth) function}. Each application of the Shapley value requires an appropriate game function. In  \cite{Shapley_Tuple_Bertossi}, it was applied with the query itself as the game function, to quantify the contribution of tuples to a query answer. \ In \cite{Shapley_Tuple_Bertossi}, the same was done with the {Banzhaf Power-Index} \cite{banzhaf_value}.

    {\sf CES} has its origin in  the {\em causal effect} measure of {\em statistical causality and observational studies} \cite{rubin,holland}, where one usually cannot predefine and build control groups, but they have to be recovered from the available data  \cite{gelman,Pearl_CI,roy&salimi23}.  It  was first  applied   as an explanation score in data management in \cite{Causal_Effect}, and extended to general PDBs and investigated in terms of general properties in \cite{clear25}. \ However, in this work we stick to its simplest version that can be applied to non-probabilistic DBs, as in  \cite{Causal_Effect}. \
    In \cite{Shapley_Tuple_Bertossi},
    it was established that  this version of {\sf CES} coincides with the Banzhaf Power-Index  as applied to DBs. We denote  this particular case with {\sf BPI}. \   The alignment of general game-theoretic scores, e.g. of the Shapley Value and the Banzhaf Power-Index, has been investigated under ``ordinal equivalence" by the game-theory community \cite{freixas,freixas12}. \ However, the results  do not directly apply to our case.

In actual causality, counterfactual interventions affect {\em endogenous variables}, while those variables that are not affected by them are called {\em exogenous}. In the context of DBs, this amounts to having a partition of the instance into {\em endogenous and exogenous tuples}. Interventions only affect the former, and can be thought as tuple insertions and tuple deletions. When the query at hand is monotone, e.g. a conjunctive query, only deletions matter. This kind of partition of the DB has also been considered with {\sf Resp} \cite{QA_Causality}, \ with both {\sf Shapley} and {\sf BPI} \cite{Shapley_Tuple_Bertossi}, and {\sf CES} \cite{Causal_Effect,clear25}. It turns out that the the presence of exogenous tuples has an effect on the values taken by the scores, and also on their computational properties. \ Actually, as we show in this work, exogenous tuples also affect the alignment of scores.

More specifically,  we investigate in detail conditions on DB instances and Boolean Conjunctive Queries (BCQs) under which pairs of scores are aligned or not. In fact, we will unveil some syntactic classes of queries for which the scores are always aligned; and also, other classes for which  the scores are not always aligned. The role or presence of exogenous tuples in the DB turns out to be relevant.

In this work, {\sf CES} takes a prominent role. It has been less investigated by the data management community than the other scores. It was first proposed and used in \cite{Causal_Effect}, but its properties -actually in a generalized version- have been investigated only recently in \cite{clear25}, where its complexity and axiomatic properties have been established. \ In \cite{Causal_Effect},  {\sf CES}  was introduced as an  alternative to {\sf Resp}. In this work, we pay special attention to the alignment of these two scores.

We first compare  {\sf CES} with {\sf Resp} in two scenarios: (a) Instances with exogenous tuples, and (b) Instances without them. \ For case (a), we fully characterize the class of BCQs, that is, for every BCQ, we can determine if the scores are always aligned or not. \ For case (b), we exhibit classes of queries for which the scores are always aligned. \ Finally, we compare  {\sf CES} and {\sf Resp} with {\sf Shapley}, exhibiting a class of queries for which the scores are not aligned.

This paper is structured as follows. \  In Section \ref{sec:back}, we provide necessary background, including {\sf Resp}, {\sf Shapley} and {\sf BPI};   and related work. \ In Section \ref{sec:CE}, we present the {\em Generalized Causal-Effect Score} on PDBs recently introduced in \cite{clear25}, and {\sf CES} as a particular case. \ In Section \ref{sec:alig}, we show  motivating examples of (non-)alignment; we define alignment; and also present some basic and unifying properties of the scores that are needed later in the paper. \ In Section \ref{sec:ces-resp}, the main section, we investigate the alignment of {\sc CES} and {\sf Resp}. \ In Section \ref{sec:ces-shap}, we bring {\sf Shapley} into the alignment analysis.
    \ In Section \ref{sec:concl}, we draw some conclusions and point to future work. The Appendix contains proofs for all results.


\section{Background} \label{sec:back}




\noindent {\bf 1. \ Databases.} \   A relational schema $\mc{R}$ contains a domain of constants, $\mc{C}$,  and a set of  predicates of finite arities, $\mc{P}$. \ $\mc{R}$ gives rise to a language $\mf{L}(\mc{R})$ of first-order (FO)  predicate logic.  Variables are  denoted with $x, y, z, ...$; and finite sequences thereof with $\bar{x}, ...$; and constants with $a, b, c, ...$, etc. An {\em atom} is of the form $P(t_1, \ldots, t_n)$, with $n$-ary $P \in \mc{P}$   and $t_1, \ldots, t_n$ are constants  or variables.
  A {\em tuple} is  a {\em ground} atom, i.e. without  variables. A database (instance), $D$, for $\mc{R}$ is a finite set of ground atoms that serves as an interpretation structure for  $\mf{L}(\mc{R})$. \ The {\em active domain} of $D$, denoted ${\it Adom}(D)$, is the set of constants that appear in atoms of $D$. We adopt a set-semantics for DBs.



A {\em conjunctive query} \ (CQ), $\mc{Q}(\bar{x})$,  is an existentially quantified conjunction of atoms:  \ $\exists  \bar{y}\;(P_1(\bar{x}_1)\wedge \dots \wedge P_m(\bar{x}_m))$, whose
 free variables are $\bar{x} := (\bigcup \bar{x}_i) \smallsetminus \bar{y}$. If $\mc{Q}$ has $n$ free variables,  $\bar{c} \in \mc{C}^n$ \ is an {\em answer} to $\mc{Q}$ from $D$ if $D \models \mc{Q}[\bar{c}]$, i.e.  $Q[\bar{c}]$ is true in $D$  when the variables in $\bar{x}$ are componentwise replaced by the values in $\bar{c}$. $\mc{Q}[D]$ denotes the set of answers to $\mc{Q}$ from $D$. \ $\mc{Q}$ is a {\em Boolean CQ}  when $\bar{x}$ is empty. For a Boolean query (BQ),  when it is {\em true} in $D$,  $\mc{Q}[D] := \{1\}$. Otherwise, if it is {\em false}, $\mc{Q}[D] := \{0\}$. \
 Alternatively, we write $\mc{Q}[D] =1$ and $\mc{Q}[D] =0$, resp., which allows us to treat BQs as numeric queries. \
\ This paper concentrates on {\em Monotone BQs} (MBQs),  in particular, BCQs. For MBQs it holds, by definition, $\mc{Q}[D] \leq \mc{Q}[D^\prime]$ when $D \subseteq D^\prime$.
\  For a BCQ $\mc{Q}$ and a variable $v$ in it,  $\nit{Atoms}(v)$ denotes the set of atoms in $\mc{Q}$ where $v$ appears; and $\nit{Atoms}(\mc{Q})$ denotes the set of all atoms in $\mc{Q}$. \ignore{++  A BCQ query $\mc{Q}$ is {\em hierarchical} if, for any two variables $x, y$ in it, one of the following holds \cite{suciu}: (a) $\nit{Atoms}(x) \subseteq \nit{Atoms}(y)$, (b) $\nit{Atoms}(y) \subseteq \nit{Atoms}(y)$ or (c) $\nit{Atoms}(x) \cap \nit{Atoms}(y) = \emptyset$. \ Otherwise, the query is called \emph{non-hierarchical} \cite{suciu}.++}



\vspace{1mm}
\noindent {\bf 2. \ Actual Causality and Responsibility in Databases.} \
 For causality purposes, some of the tuples in a DB $D$ are  {\em endogenous}, and can be subject to   {\em counterfactual interventions}, in this case, deletions, insertions, or value updates. The other tuples are {\em exogenous}, and are taken as a given, leaving them outside a specific kind of analysis, but they participate in query answering. \ The partition $D = D^\nit{en} \cup D^\nit{ex}$ is application dependent.

 \ignore{++ To simplify the presentation, and unless otherwise stated, we will assume that all the tuples in $(A(\mc{S},D) \smallsetminus D^\nit{en})$ are exogenous.  \ We will denote with $\nit{Var}^{\!\nit{en}\!}(\mc{L}_D^{\!\mc{Q}})$ and $\nit{Var}^{\!\nit{ex}\!}(\mc{L}_D^{\!\mc{Q}})$ the sets of propositional variables corresponding to endogenous and exogenous tuples, respectively. ++}

  \ A tuple $\tau \in D^\nit{en}$ is a {\em counterfactual cause}   for a BQ $\mc{Q}$ if: $D \models \mc{Q}$, and $(D \smallsetminus \{\tau\}) \not \models \mc{Q}$. \ A tuple $\tau \in D^\nit{en}$ is an {\em actual cause}   for $\mc{Q}$ if there is $\Gamma \subseteq D^\nit{en}$, such that:  $D \models \mc{Q}$, \ $(D \smallsetminus \Gamma) \models \mc{Q}$, and $(D \smallsetminus (\Gamma \cup \{\tau\})) \not \models \mc{Q}$ \ \cite{QA_Causality}.
\ The set $\Gamma$ is called a {\em contingency set} for $\tau$. $\nit{Cont}(D,\mc{Q},\tau)$, or simply, $\nit{Cont}(D,\tau)$, denotes the set of contingency sets for $\tau$.

The {\em responsibility} of $\tau$ as an actual cause for $\mc{Q}$ is defined by \ $\rho(D,\mc{Q},\tau) := \frac{1}{1 + |\Gamma|}$,
	where \(|\Gamma|\) is the cardinality of a minimum-size contingency set for \(\tau\) \ \cite{chockler,QA_Causality}. (We sometimes write $\rho(D,\tau)$ or $\rho(\tau)$.) \ A counterfactual cause is an actual cause with maximum responsibility,  $1$. \ If $\tau$ is not an actual cause, its responsibility is defined as $0$.
 Responsibility as used in DBs will be  denoted with {\sf Resp}. It quantifies the causal contribution of a tuple to a query answer \cite{QA_Causality,tocs,flairs17}.

\vspace{1mm}
     \noindent {\bf 3. \ Shapley Value and Banzhaf Power-Index.} \  For their application in DBs, a BQ $\mc{Q}$ becomes the game function   \cite{Shapley_Tuple_Bertossi,sigRec21,sigRec23}:

    \vspace{-6mm}
    \begin{eqnarray}
    \nit{Shapley}(D,\mc{Q},\tau) \  &=& \!\!\!\! \sum_{S \subseteq (D^\nit{en} \smallsetminus \{\tau\})} \!\!\! \!\!\dfrac{|S|! \cdot (|D^\nit{en}| - |S| - 1)!}{|D^\nit{en}|!}  \cdot \Delta(\mc{Q},S,\tau), \label{eq:shap}\\
     \nit{BPI}(D,\mc{Q},\tau) \ &:=& \! \! \sum_{S \subseteq D^\nit{en} \smallsetminus \{\tau\}} \!\! \dfrac{1}{2^{|D^\nit{en}|-1}} \cdot \Delta(\mc{Q},S,\tau), \ \  \mbox{ with }\label{eq:banzhaf}\\
       \Delta(\mc{Q},S,\tau) \ &:=& \ \ \mc{Q}[S \cup D^\nit{ex} \cup \{\tau\}] \ - \  \mc{Q}[S \cup D^\nit{ex}]. \label{eq:delta}
    \end{eqnarray}
   Since $\mc{Q}$ is Boolean, $\mc{Q}[S] \in \{0,1\}$.
\ The definition of the Shapley value guarantees that {\em it is the only measure} of players' contributions to the coalition game's shared wealth function  that satisfies certain desirable properties \cite{shapley1953original,roth1988shapley}. Those properties provide a categorical axiomatization for the Shapley value. There is also a  categorical axiomatization for the BPI \cite{BPI_Math_Props}. \ In the following we will denote these attribution scores in DBs with {\sf Shapley} and {\sf BPI}, resp.

\vspace{1mm}
 \noindent {\bf 4. \ Probabilistic Databases.}
   \ Given a relational DB, $D$, a PDB, $D^p$ associated to $D$ can be conceived as the {\em collection $\mc{W}$ of possible worlds}, i.e. of all subinstances $W$ of  $D$; and each $W \in \mc{W}$ has a  probability $p(W)$, such that  $\sum_{W\in\mc{W}}p(W) = 1$ \cite{suciu}. \ Accordingly, $D^p$  can be identified with a discrete {\em probability space} $\langle \mc{W}, p\rangle$, with $p$ probability distribution on $\mc{W}$. \ The probability of tuple $\tau$ being in $D$ (seen through the PDB $D^p$) is  \
$P(\tau) \ := \sum_{\tau \in W \in \mc{W}} p(W)$. %\label{eq:tupProb}
\
Since, $D$ is finite, $\mc{W}$ and the $W$ are all finite.
\ A numeric query $\mc{Q}$ on $D$ becomes a random variable on  $\mc{W}$; with Bernoulli distribution if it is a BQ,  in which case, the probability of $\mc{Q}$ (being true) is \
$P(\mc{Q})  := P(\mc{Q} = 1) :=
\sum_{W \in \mc{W}: \ W \models \mc{Q}} p(W)$. \ignore{
If $\mc{Q}(\bar{x})$ is an open query, and $\bar{a}$, a sequence of constants, \ $P(\bar{a}) := P(\mc{Q}[\bar{a}])$.
\ Under this semantics,  each answer comes with a probability (of being an answer).}
\ For a Boolean query, \
$P(\mc{Q}) = \mbb{E}(\mc{Q})$.


 \  The  particular case of a {\em tuple-independent PDB} (TID)
 is a regular relational DB whose  relations have an extra attribute  with probabilities for the tuples. Tuples are stochastically independent from each other. \
 Table \ref{tab:pdbs} shows a TID. \ The first tuple, $\tau_1$,  is in the DB with probability $p_1$. \ For a possible world $W \in \mc{W}$, the corresponding (non-probabilistic) relation $R_W$ will contain only some of the tuples in $R$, and has the probability: \ $p(R_W) := \Pi_{_{\tau_i \in R_W}} p_i \ \times \ \Pi_{_{\tau_j \in (R \smallsetminus R_W)}} (1-p_j)$.

