    \section{The Causal-Effect Score in Databases}
    \label{sec:CE}


    We define in this section the {\em Causal Effect Score} (CES) in DBs in the form that we need it. The definition is equivalent to that in \cite{Causal_Effect}, but differs from it in that the CES now becomes a  particular case of the {\em Generalized Causal-Effect Score} recently proposed and investigated in \cite{clear25}.  In the following, $\tau$ is a an endogenous tuple in relational DB instance $D$, and $\mc{W}$ is the class of all subinstances of $D$.\footnote{In \cite{clear25}, the generalized causal-effect is defined on an arbitrary collection of subinstances of $D$.} \ With a PDB $D^p$ associated to  $D$ via a distribution $p$ on $\mc{W}$, \ tuple $\tau$ has a certain probability of being (true) in $D$. \ Exogenous tuples have probability $1$ of being in $D$.



\ignore{\subsection{Interventions Database  Distributions} }

CES is defined through {\em  interventions} on the distribution of the PDB $D^p$. They are of the forms $\nit{do}(\tau \In)$ and $\nit{do}(\tau \Out)$, with the intuitive meaning that tuple $\tau$ is {\em  made true}, i.e. it is forced to be in the database  at hand. Similarly,  $\nit{do}(\tau \Out)$ means that $\tau$ is {\em made false}, i.e. forced not to be in  the DB.



 We use expressions of the form  $P(\mc{Q}=1~|~\nit{do}(\tau \In))$, where $P$ is the distribution induced by $p$ on the BQ $\mc{Q}$ treated as a random variable on $\mc{W}$. Intuitively, it means ``the probability of the query being true given that tuple $\tau$ is made true".  More precisely, the seeming ``conditionalizations" on $\nit{do}(\tau \In)$ and $\nit{do}(\tau \Out)$ should be seen as perturbations of the original distribution $p$ on $\mc{W}$.





 \begin{definition}  \label{def:DO} \em Consider an instance $D = D^\nit{en} \cup D^\nit{ex}$,   an associated PDB $D^p=\langle \mc{W}, p\rangle$, $\tau \in D^\nit{en}$. The interventions on  $D^p$ are the new PDBs:

 \vspace{1mm} \noindent  (a) \ $D^p(\nit{do}(\tau \In)) := \langle \mc{W},p^{+\tau}\rangle$: \ For $W \in \mc{W}$, \ \ $p^{+\tau}(W)  \ := \sum_{W^\prime \in \mc{W} \ : \ W^\prime \cup \{\tau\} = W}  p(W^\prime)$.

 \vspace{1mm} \noindent (b) \ $D^p(\nit{do}(\tau \Out)) := \langle \mc{W},p^{-\tau}\rangle$: \ For $W \in \mc{W}$, \ \
  $p^{-\tau}(W) \ := \sum_{W^\prime \in \mc{W} \ : \ W^\prime \smallsetminus \{\tau\} = W}  p(W^\prime)$.

\vspace{1mm}
  \noindent
  (c) \ For a BQ $\mc{Q}$, and $v \in \{0,1\}$: \ \  $P(\mc{Q}=v~|~\nit{do}(\tau \mbox{\phantom{o}}\In)) \ := \
p^{+\tau}( \{W \in \mc{W}~|~\mc{Q}[W]=v\} )$.

\hspace*{4.3cm}$
P(\mc{Q}=v~|~\nit{do}(\tau \Out)) \ := \
p^{-\tau}( \{W \in \mc{W}~|~ \mc{Q}[W] =v\})$.
\boxtheorem
\end{definition}

