This definition of intervened distribution was first given in \cite{clear25}, and can be applied to arbitrary PDBs, and not only to TIDs.  \ In \cite{Causal_Effect} (see also  \cite{bda22}), for the case of DBs with associated TIDs, interventions are applied on the {\em lineage of the query}, which can be seen  as a causal model. (It is common to apply interventions on variables of a causal model \cite{Pearl_CI}.) Those interventions on the query lineage act on the random propositional variables $X_\tau$ associated to tuples $\tau$, which are made {\em true} (inserted) or {\em false} (deleted) via $\nit{do}(X_\tau =1)$ or  $\nit{do}(X_\tau =0)$ .



A {\em Generalized Causal-Effect Score} (GCES) was defined and investigated in \cite{clear25}. Here, we  concentrate on the particular case  in Definition \ref{def:gce}, that provably coincides with that  in \cite{Causal_Effect}.


    \begin{definition} \label{def:gce} \em
        Let $D$ be a relational instance with an associated $U(\frac{1}{2})$-TID $D^p = $  $\langle \mc{W},$ $p \rangle$, and
        $\mc{Q}$ a BQ. \ The {\em causal-effect score}, denoted  {\sf CES}, of $\tau \in  D^{\nit{en}}$ on $\mc{Q}$ is:

        \vspace{2mm}
        \hspace*{2cm}$\nit{CE}(D,\mc{Q},\tau) \ := \ \mathbb{E}(\mc{Q}~|~ \nit{do}(\tau \In)) - \mathbb{E}(\mc{Q}~|~\nit{do}(\tau \Out))$. %\label{eq:gce}
        \boxtheorem
    \end{definition}

We  emphasize that {\sf CES} uses an auxiliary, intermediate PDB, but the original DB is not probabilistic, and {\sf CES} is not a probabilistic score in that the value is not a probability. \  {\sf Resp}, {\sf Shapley} and {\sf BPI} of Section \ref{sec:back} are not probabilistic scores, and they do not use an intermediate PDB.

\ignore{Furthermore, the use of the CES (generalized or not) for aggregate queries is much more sensible and natural than applying  {\sf Resp} \cite{clear25}. This is because, in contrast to BQs, for which a counterfactual intervention may switch its value  to $1$ or $0$, with an aggregate query, we have the issue of deciding what is a reasonable margin of change in the aggregated amount.}

{\sf CES} captures the expected difference in the query value between  having the tuple in the DB and not having it. Each of the expectations come from the fact that all the possible combinations (actually, possible worlds) of the other tuples are considered for query answering, each combination making the query true or false with a certain probability.
 \ The score in Definition \ref{def:gce} can be generalized and applied to arbitrary PDBs and numeric queries \cite{clear25}. However, we will consider only the  case in Definition \ref{def:gce}.

For a BQ, it holds: \ $\nit{CE}(D,\mc{Q},\tau) = \sum_{v \in \{0,1\}} v \times P(\mc{Q} =v~|~\nit{do}(\tau \In)) - \sum_{v \in \{0,1\}} v \times P(\mc{Q} =v~|~\nit{do}(\tau \Out)) =
P(\mc{Q} = 1~|~\nit{do}(\tau \In)) - P(\mc{Q} = 1~|~\nit{do}(\tau \Out))$.









