       If this is the case, I'm OK with this.
       }}

        \noindent
        (b) \ Numeric scores ${\sf sc_1}, \ {\sf sc_2}$ \ are \emph{aligned  for} $(\mc{Q},D)$ if, for every $\tau,\tau^\prime \in D^\nit{en}$, $\tau \preceq^{\sf sc_1} \tau^\prime$ iff $\tau \preceq^{\sf sc_2} \tau^\prime$. \ \ We say that ${\sf sc_1}, \ {\sf sc_2}$ are {\em aligned  for $\mc{Q}$} \
       when, for every instance $D$ compatible with $\mc{Q}$, they are aligned for $(\mc{Q},D)$.

        \vspace{1mm}\noindent
        (c) \  The preorders for {\sf CES}, {\sf Resp} and {\sf Shapley} are denoted with \ $\preceq^{\nit{CE}}$, \ $\preceq^\rho$, and \ $\preceq^{\nit{Sh}}$, resp. \boxtheorem
\end{definition}

    According to Definition \ref{def:rank_alignment}, if a score assigns the same value to all tuples, it will be aligned with any other score. In Example \ref{ex:paths_detailedNEW}, {\sf Resp} is aligned with {\sf CES} and {\sf Shapley}.  However,  for other monotone queries, these three scores may not be pairwise aligned. That is, for every pair of scores, there is a pair $(\mc{Q},D)$ for which the rankings are incompatible, as the  counterexamples below show.

