    \begin{proposition} \em
        \label{prop:resp_mss_disjoint}
        Let $D$ be a database instance and $\mc{Q}$ a Boolean monotone query. Let $\nit{MSS}\!(D,\mc{Q})$ be the collection of minimal satisfiability sets (c.f. Definition \ref{def:swinging_world}). If, for every $W$ and $W^\prime \in \nit{MSS}(D,\mc{Q}), \ W \cap W^\prime = \emptyset$, then, the \emph{Responsibility} of a tuple $\tau \in D^\nit{en}$ is:
        \[
        \rho(D,\mc{Q},\tau) = \begin{cases}
            \dfrac{1}{|\nit{MSS}(D,\mc{Q})|} & \text{, if } \tau \in W \text{ for some } W \in \nit{MSS}(D,\mc{Q})\\
            0 & \text{, otherwise}
        \end{cases}
        \]
    \end{proposition}
    \begin{proof}
        Note that, if there is no $W \in \nit{MSS}(D,\mc{Q})$ such $\tau \in W$, no contingency set exists for $\tau$, and therefore $\rho(D,\mc{Q},\tau) = 0$.

        Now, consider $\tau \in W$, for some $W \in \nit{MSS}(D,\mc{Q})$. Let $\Gamma^\star(\tau)$ denote the collection of set $\Gamma$ that selects one endogenous tuple from each $W^\prime \in \nit{MSS}(D,\mc{Q}) \smallsetminus W$. The cardinality of any of those sets is: $|\nit{MSS}(D,\mc{Q})| - 1$.

        It is clear that any set $\Gamma \in \Gamma^\star(\tau)$ is a contingency for the tuple $\tau$, and if any tuple is removed from $\Gamma$, then it would not be a contingency of $\tau$. It follows that any of such sets has the minimal cardinality, since for any other contingency $\Gamma^\prime \not\in \Gamma^\star(\tau)$, $\Gamma^\prime$ is a superset of some contingency in $\Gamma^\star(\tau)$. Therefore, $\rho(D,\mc{Q},\tau) = \frac{1}{|\nit{MSS}(D,\mc{Q})|}$. {\hfill \boxtheorem}
    \end{proof}
