\section{{\sf CES} and {\sf Resp} Alignment} \label{sec:ces-resp}


Here, we investigate the alignment of  {\sf CES} and {\sf Resp}, with  the query BCQ $\mc{Q}$ as our main parameter. That is,  under what conditions on $\mc{Q}$, the scores are aligned for $\mc{Q}$ \ {\em for every instance}; and whether there is an instance $D$ for which they are not aligned (see Definition \ref{def:rank_alignment}(b)).  \
    We will need to consider the presence or not of exogenous tuples.  \  In Section \ref{sub:ces-resp_noex}, we investigate alignment in the absence of exogenous tuples. Until that section, we allow instances to have them.
\    We need to start with some  definitions about the syntactic structure of a query.



 \begin{definition} \em \label{def:component_coinvar}
      \ (query components and coincident variables) \  Let $\mc{Q}$ be a BCQ.

      \noindent  (a) The undirected graph $G$ associated to $\mc{Q}$ has as nodes the atoms of $\mc{Q}$, and its edges are the pairs of atoms, $(A_i,A_j)$, that have at least one variable in common.

      \noindent  (b) The {\em components} of $\mc{Q}$ are the connected components of  $G$, usually denoted with $C_1,\ldots,C_n$.

      \noindent   (c) For \ $i=1, \ldots, n$, \ $\mc{Q}_i$ denotes the {\em associated subquery} of $\mc{Q}$ that is the existential closure of the conjunction of the atoms in component $C_i$.

      \noindent  (d)  $V\subseteq  \nit{Var}(\mc{Q})$ is a \emph{coincident set of variables} if  the sets $\nit{Atoms}(v)$, with $v \in V$, are the same. \ The set of all (maximal) coincident sets of variables is denoted with $\nit{Coin}(\mc{Q})$; and its elements are called {\em coincidences}. \ Coincidences that are singletons are said to be {\em trivial coincidences}. \boxtheorem
    \end{definition}

  \vspace{-2mm}  \begin{example} \label{ex:coin} \
        For the query $\mc{Q}\!:\! \exists x \exists y \exists z \exists w (R(x,y)\land S(x) \land T(z,w)\land U(z))$, its associated  graph $G$ has as nodes the elements of $\nit{Atoms}(\mc{Q}) = \{R(x,y), S(x), T(z,w), U(z)\}$. The edges are the pairs $(R(x,y),S(x))$ and  $(T(z,w),U(w))$, and the  two components are: \ $C_1 = \{R(x,y),S(x)\}$ and $C_2 = \{T(z,w),U(w)\}$. Their associated subqueries  are $\mc{Q}_1\!: \exists x \exists y (R(x,y) \land S(x))$ and $\mc{Q}_2 \!: \exists z \exists w (T(z,w) \land U(z))$.
\  $\nit{Atom}(x) = \{R(x,y), S(x)\}$, and $\nit{Atom}(y) = \{ R(x,y)\}$. The two sets are different, then $x$ and $y$ are not coincident: \ $\nit{Coin}(\mc{Q}) = \{\{x\}, \{y\}, \{z\}, \{w\}\}$ contains only trivial coincidences. \
         $\mc{Q}^\prime\!:~\exists x \exists y\exists z(R(x,y) \wedge T(x,y,z) \wedge U(z))$ has a single component, and  \ $\nit{Coin}(\mc{Q}^\prime) = \{\{x,y\},$ $ \{z\}\}$ contains the non-trivial coincidence $\{x,y\}$.
        \boxtheorem
    \end{example}

\vspace{1mm} Notice that: (a) $\nit{Coin}(\mc{Q})$ is a partition of $\nit{Var}(\mc{Q})$. \ (b)  If $\mc{Q}$ contains only trivial coincident sets, i.e. singletons, then $|\nit{Coin}(\mc{Q})| = |\nit{Var}(\mc{Q})|$, as  in Example \ref{ex:coin}. \
    (c) Any two components of a query do not share any variable.
\  Components can be identified with ``independent" sub-queries.

Variables in the same coincident set can be treated as if they were only one and the same (see \ Proposition \ref{prop:coinc} in Section \ref{sec:multiple}). We  will  take advantage of this when dealing with queries with multiple components. \ We concentrate next on single-component queries with only trivial coincidences. In Section \ref{sec:multiple}, results for them will be used for general BCQs.
 \ {\em To simplify the presentation, we will assume that queries do not have constants. Our results still hold if they have them (see Remark \ref{rem:last} in the Appendix).}

