\subsection{Single-Component Boolean Conjunctive Queries and Trivial Coincidences}\label{sec:single}

We start here with Propositions \ref{proposition:ces_resp_1} and \ref{proposition:ces_resp_2} that  give us, resp., particular  alignment and non-alignment results. We will use them  later on to obtain more general results. \ Notice that the query in the following proposition has a single component, and only trivial coincidences.

 \begin{proposition} \em \label{proposition:ces_resp_1}  %\color{blue}
        Consider the query $\mc{Q}_{R_n}\!\!\!: \exists x (R_1(x) \land R_2(x) \land \cdots \land R_n(x))$, with
        $n \geq 1$. \  {\sf CES} and {\sf Resp} are aligned for  $(\mc{Q}_{R_n},D)$, for every instance $D$ with or without exogenous tuples. \boxtheorem
    \end{proposition}

  \vspace{-3mm}\begin{remark} \label{rem:technique} \ In order to establish negative, i.e. non-alignment, results, for a given class, $\mc{C}$, of queries, we will use on several occasions the following idea and technique:\ignore{\footnote{A similar technique was used in \cite{Shapley_Tuple_Bertossi,dichotomy_CQ}.}}

  \noindent (a) \ Start with a particular example (a counterexample) consisting in a particular query $\mc{Q}$ and a particular instance $D_0$, for which the negative result holds. The particular query belongs to $\mc{C}$, and is, in some sense, ``contained" in the other queries in the class.

  \noindent  (b) \ Given an arbitrary query $\mc{Q}^\prime$ in $\mc{C}$, query $\mc{Q}$ is reconstructed as a part of $\mc{Q}^\prime$.

  \noindent  (c) \ In order to obtain an instance
    $D$ for $\mc{Q}^\prime$ (where there is no alignment),  we start from $D_0$, making
     $D$ an extension of $D_0$  by adding exogenous tuples to the latter.
     \ In this way, every tuple in $D_0$ has a corresponding tuple in $D$. The new tuples in $D$ will be endogenous iff the original tuples in $D_0$ are endogenous.

    \noindent (d) \ Tuples in $D$ corresponding to tuples in $D_0$, have the same {\sf CES} and {\sf Resp} scores as in $D_0$. \boxtheorem
    \end{remark}

   \vspace{2mm} Proposition \ref{proposition:ces_resp_2} is obtained with this technique. The required counterexample is Example \ref{ex:counter_q2}.


