\subsection{Dealing with Non-Trivial Coincidences}\label{sec:single+}


 The results of Section \ref{sec:single} are for single-component queries without non-trivial coincidences. In this section, we introduce techniques for dealing with BCQs with non-trivial coincidences. With them, we will be able to extend results from the previous section to general BCQs, in Section \ref{sec:multiple}. For that, we need Proposition \ref{lemma:coin_var} at the end of this section. \ignore{below, where $\mc{Q}^\nit{red}$ and $D^\nit{red}$ are the \emph{reduced forms} of $\mc{Q}$ and $D$, resp. (see Remark \ref{rem:red})}

 \begin{proposition} \label{prop:coinc} \em (mapping coincident variables) \
        Let $\mc{Q}$ be a  self-join-free BCQ for a schema $\mc{S}$. Let  $x,y \in \nit{Var}(\mc{Q})$ be  such that $\nit{Atoms}(x) = \nit{Atoms}(y)$. Consider the BCQ query $\mc{Q}^\prime$ for a new schema $\mc{S}^\prime$ obtained by decreasing the arity of the relational predicates of atoms in $\nit{Atoms}(x)$ by $1$, and replacing the occurrences of $x,y$ by a single occurrence of a fresh variable $v$. \ Then, \ for every instance $D$ for $\mc{S}$, one can build an instance $D^\prime$ for $\mc{S}^\prime$ via a linear-time computable transformation $f$ of tuples in $D$, such that, for every $S \subseteq D$: \ $\mc{Q}[S] = \mc{Q}^\prime[f(S)]$. \boxtheorem
    \end{proposition}

 We illustrate the proposition and  its proof by means of  an example.



     \begin{example} \label{ex:red} Consider \ $\mc{Q}\!: \  \exists x \exists y \exists x (R(x,y) \wedge S(y,z,x) \wedge T(z))$, and the instance $D$ in Table \ref{tab:coincident}, with $D^\nit{ex} =\{\tau_1,\tau_2\}$. \
           The only non-trivial equality of sets of atoms for any two variables is $\nit{Atoms}(x)$ $ = \nit{Atoms}(y)$. \ Accordingly, we ``identify" $x$ and $y$, or make them collapse into a single variable, $v$, with  corresponding attribute, $A^\prime$. \ The new schema is \ $\mc{S}^\prime = \{R^\prime(\cdot), T^\prime(\cdot,\cdot),$ $ U(\cdot)\}$, and the new query is \
$\mc{Q}^\prime\!: \ \exists v \exists z (R^\prime(v) \wedge T^\prime(v,z) \wedge U(z))$. \ With this query, we make decrease the cardinality of the two-element coincidence from $2$, for $\{x,y\}$, to $1$, for $\{v\}$.



