 \vspace{1mm}  As the previous example shows, in a self-join-free query, each relation appears only once, so the variable  ordering is irrelevant. If a relation appears more than once, with different variable orders, collapsing variables can lead to inconsistent mappings, as shown in the next example.

    \begin{example}
        Consider $D = \{R(a,b),R(c,c)\}$, and $\mc{Q}_{\sf sj} \!:\ \exists x \exists y (R(x,y) \land R(y,x))$ that has a   self-join. Here, $\atoms(x) = \atoms(y)$. We collapse $x$ and $y$ into a single variable $v$, with a new relation $R^\prime(\cdot)$, resulting, with the mapping $f : D \to D^\prime$ defined by $f(R(a,b)) = R^\prime(c_1)$ and $f(R(c,c)) = R^\prime(c_2)$, in: (a) A new query $\mc{Q}^\prime_{\sf sj}\!:\ \exists v(R^\prime(v) \land (R^\prime(v))$; and (2) A new instance $D^\prime = \{R^\prime(c_1),R^\prime(c_2)\}$. \
        It is clear that $\mc{Q}_{\sf sj}[\{R(a,b)\}] = 0 \neq \mc{Q}^\prime_{\sf sj}[\{f(R(a,b))\}] = \mc{Q}^\prime_{\sf sj}[\{R^\prime(c_1)\}] = 1$. This  shows that, for queries with self-joins, the mapping may fail to preserve query equivalence.
    \boxtheorem \end{example}


    \begin{remark} \label{rem:red}
    (a) \ {\em For now on, we will consider only self-join-free BCQs.}

    %\vspace{1mm}
    \noindent
    (b) \ For simplicity, Proposition \ref{prop:coinc} presents the case of two variables in a coincidence. However, the result can be generalized to all the variables in a coincidence by iteratively decreasing the coincidence until its size becomes $1$.

    %\vspace{1mm}
    \noindent (c) \
    For a BCQ $\mc{Q}$ with a non-trivial coincidence, the \emph{reduced form} of $\mc{Q}$, denoted by $\mc{Q}^\nit{red}$, is the result of iteratively decreasing the number of coincident variables, until making each $V \in \nit{Coin}(\mc{Q})$ collapse into a singleton. \  $\mc{Q}^\nit{red}$ does not have any non-trivial coincidence; then $\nit{Coin}(\mc{Q}^\nit{red}) = \nit{Var}(\mc{Q}^\nit{red})$. \ In Example \ref{ex:red}, $\mc{Q}^\prime$ is the \emph{reduced form} of $\mc{Q}$.
\ Similarly, $D^\nit{red}$ denotes the {\em reduced form} of $D$, obtained by applying the one-step reduction $f^\nit{red}$ that is the terminating iterative application (self-composition) of $f$ to $D$.

   % \vspace{1mm}
    \noindent
(d) Mapping $f$ assigns, to each unique combination of constants that occurs in the input instance in the positions of the coincident variables,  a new fresh constant in a target instance. \ The schema and data transformations depend on the query at hand.

 \vspace{1mm}\noindent
    (e) \  The set of exogenous tuples is preserved, that is, a tuple in the target instance is exogenous iff its corresponding tuple in the original instance is exogenous.
    \boxtheorem
        \end{remark}




    \begin{proposition} \label{lemma:coin_var} \em
        Let $\mc{Q}$ be a self-join-free BCQ, possibly with non-trivial coincidences. For every instance $D$, \ {\sf CES} and {\sf Resp} are aligned for  $(\mc{Q},D)$ iff they are aligned for $(\mc{Q}^\nit{red}, D^\nit{red})$. \boxtheorem
    \end{proposition}





     We illustrate the use of Proposition \ref{lemma:coin_var} to obtain alignment and non-alignment results.

    \begin{example}
        Consider  BCQs $\mc{Q}$ and $\mc{Q}^\prime$, with their \emph{reduced versions},  $\mc{Q}^{\nit{red}}$ and $\mc{Q}^{\prime^{\nit{red}}}$\!, resp.:

       \vspace{1mm}
        \noindent $\mc{Q}\!: \ \exists x \exists y \exists z(R(x,y,z)\wedge S(x,y,z)\wedge T(x,y,z))$, \hfill $\mc{Q}^{\nit{red}}\!: \  \exists v(R(v)\wedge S(v) \wedge T(v))$,\\
        $\mc{Q}^\prime\!: \ \exists x \exists y \exists w(R(x,y,w)\wedge S(x,y)\wedge T(x,y))$, \hfill $\mc{Q}^{\prime^{\nit{red}}}\!\!\!\!: \ \exists v^\prime \exists w (R(v^\prime,w)\wedge S(v^\prime)\wedge T(v^\prime))$.

       \vspace{1mm}
        By Proposition \ref{proposition:ces_resp_1},  {\sf CES} and {\sf Resp} are aligned for $(\mc{Q}^\nit{red}D^\nit{red})$, for every instance $D^\nit{red}$. \ From Proposition \ref{lemma:coin_var}, it follows that the scores are aligned for $(\mc{Q},D)$, for every instance $D$.

 By Proposition \ref{proposition:ces_resp_2}, there is an instance $D^{\prime^{\nit{red}}}$\!\!, such that {\sf CES} and {\sf Resp} are not aligned for $(\mc{Q}^{\prime^{\nit{red}}},D^{\prime^{\nit{red}}})$. Again, by Proposition \ref{lemma:coin_var}, there is an instance $D^\prime$, such that {\sf CES} and {\sf Resp} are not aligned for $(\mc{Q}^\prime,D^\prime)$.
        \boxtheorem
    \end{example}


   \subsection{General Boolean Conjunctive Queries}\label{sec:multiple}

    Now we address the general case where queries may have multiple components and any kind of coincidences. \
    We start by stating the main result of this section. Its proof is obtained from the propositions at the end of the section.



    \begin{theorem} \em \label{theo:ces_resp_aligned}
        Let $\mc{Q}$ be a BCQ, \ with components $C_1,\ldots,C_n$, and their associated queries $\mc{Q}_{i}$, as in Definition \ref{def:component_coinvar}. \
        It holds:

        \vspace{1mm} \noindent (a) \ If $n=1$ and $|\nit{Coin}(\mc{Q})| = 1$, or if $n \geq 2$ and $|\nit{Atoms}(\mc{Q}_i)| = 1$ for every $i = 1,\ldots,n$, \ then {\sf CES} and  {\sf Resp} are aligned for $(\mc{Q},D)$ for every instance $D$  with or without exogenous tuples.

        \vspace{1mm} \noindent (b) \ Otherwise, there is an instance $D$ with  exogenous tuples where {\sf CES} and {\sf Resp} are not aligned. \boxtheorem
    \end{theorem}



