    \section{Alignment of {\sf CES} and {\sf Resp} with {\sf Shapley}} \label{sec:ces-shap}

We have left so far {\sf Shapley} out of the picture in terms of a general analysis of its alignment with other scores. \   Example \ref{ex:comp_ces_shapley} showed that it is not aligned with {\sf CES} or {\sf Resp} for the particular combination $(\mc{Q}_{\sf RS},D^\star)$, with $\mc{Q_{\sf RS}} \!: \exists x \exists y \exists z (R(x,y) \land S(x,z))$. \ By appealing to the technique described in Remark \ref{rem:technique}, and using $(\mc{Q}_{\sf RS},D^\star)$, we can generalize this non-alignment result to a broader class of BCQs. \ In this section, we allow -actually, we  require- instances to have exogenous tuples.




\ignore{
    \blue{First, we shall recall Example \ref{ex:comp_ces_shapley}, which constitutes a counter-example for the two comparisons: CES and Shapley Value, and \emph{Responsibility} and \emph{Shapley Value}. Now, the objective is to generalize this counter-example by recreating the database $D^\star$ and the query $\mc{Q}_{\sf RS}$. Formally, the following proposition holds.}
    }

    \begin{proposition}\em
