\vspace{-3mm}
   Our proof of Proposition \ref{prop:ces_shap_not_aligned} requires the existence of exogenous tuples in the database. \ We do not know if the result still holds without exogenous tuples. Actually, this latter case already emerges as a problem in Game Theory -where commonly no distinction between exogenous and endogenous players is made- as the problem of the alignment (or not) of the (general) Shapley value and the Banzhaf Power-Index (that in our setting becomes {\sf CES}).  To the best of our knowledge, a complete characterization of their (non-)alignment is still an open problem  \cite{freixas}.



    \section{Conclusions} \label{sec:concl}

    Different attribution scores for tuples participating in a query answer can be used. In the database literature, one can find
applications of {\sf Resp}, {\sf Shapley}, {\sf BPI}, {\sf CES}. Each of them has been investigated in isolation. However, to the best of our knowledge, little has been done in terms of comparing them. Our work is a first systematic attempt in this direction, and concentrates on their alignment, i.e. on the problem of determining if they induce the same (or compatible) rankings of tuples. \

We have identified classes of BCQs for which {\sf Resp} and {\sf CES} are aligned, and others for which they are not. By analyzing the query syntax, we can determine score alignment as follows: \ 1. For a single-component BCQ, {\sf Resp} and {\sf CES} are aligned if the query has exactly one coincident set of variables; otherwise, they are not. \ 2. For a multi-component BCQ, the scores are aligned if each component contains only one atom; otherwise, they are not.
\ When we obtain score alignment with this test,  they are aligned for every instance, regardless of the presence of exogenous tuples. When we obtain non-alignment, there is an instance with exogenous tuples where the scores are not aligned. \ We can see that exogenous tuples become crucial. This is clearly illustrated by Example \ref{ex:new}. \ignore{For  the query $\mc{Q}_{\sf RS}$ in Example  \ref{ex:new}, there is an instance with exogenous tuples for which {\sf Resp} and {\sf CES} are not aligned (see Proposition \ref{proposition:ces_resp_2}). However, if an instance does not have exogenous tuples, these scores are aligned ().}





We have exhibited a broad class of queries for which {\sf CES} (and also {\sf Resp}) and {\sf Shap} are not aligned. \
Several classes of queries are still open in this regard, particularly for comparing {\sf Shapley} with other scores. Questions of this kind in relation to {\sf Shapley} and {\sf BPI} (and indirectly, to {\sf CES}) are still open in the general area of Game Theory.
\ Aligned scores may have very {\em different score distributions} on the tuples.  Obtaining general results  about the {\em comparison of score distributions} is an open problem.




Given that, for  some cases of queries and DBs, the scores may not be aligned, a natural question is how to decide which one to use. This important issue requires further investigation; and it is not obvious how to address it. We think that appealing to the basic properties -or better, characterizing axioms- of the scores is unavoidable. As mentioned earlier in this work, both {\sf Shapley} and {\sf BPI} have such  axiomatizations. That of {\sf BPI} is inherited by {\sf CES}. There is also an axiomatization for the generalized version of {\sf CES} \cite{clear25}.  In \cite{izza}, there is a recent axiomatization of the responsibility score for explainable machine learning. \ignore{However, to the best of our knowledge, there is none for {\sf Resp}.}  \ These general properties should shed some light on the score variability problem as well.

Properties of these scores by themselves will not solve the problem of deciding which one is the best for database applications. We need, at the same time (or even before), an agreement on what is a good score-based explanation for query answering. This  is still an open question.


