We have: \ $P(\tau_1) = 0.2 + 0.25 = 0.45$, \ $P(\tau_2) = 0.8, \ P(\tau_3) = 0.8, \ P(\tau_4) = 0.2, \ P(\tau_5) = 0, \ P(\tau_6) = 0.75$. \
Notice that  \ $P(W_1) = 0.20 \neq 0 = P(\tau_1) \times (1-P(\tau_2)) \times P(\tau_3) \times P(\tau_4) \times (1-P(\tau_5)) \times P(\tau_6)$, which shows that $D^p$ is not a TID.
\ Let us compute, e.g. the intervened distribution \ $p^{+\tau_3}(\cdot) = p(\cdot ~|~\nit{do}(\tau_3 \In))$\ignore{and  $p^{-\tau_3}(\cdot) = p(\cdot ~|~\nit{do}(\tau_3 \Out))$ on $\mc{W}$}.


\begin{eqnarray*}
