\subsection{A Data Structure Using Blocks} \label{sec:blockstructure}
Towards producing a prune-and-search style algorithm, we want some way to reduce the time required to compute the vector $t^*$ which maximizes $\textnormal{Area}((P+t^*) \cap Q)$. Instead of reducing the complexity of $P$ or $Q$ directly, we maintain and refine a specific data structure that contains information about $P$ and $Q$ and allows us to rapidly search for optimal placements.

\begin{definition}[$(\mu, b, \mathcal{T})$-Block Structure]
A \emph{$(\mu, b, \mathcal{T})$-block structure}
    is a data structure containing the following:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Access to the vertices of $P$ and $Q$ in counterclockwise order with $O(1)$ query time, and access to the area prefix sums for $P$ and $Q$ with $O(1)$ query time. 
        \item A block size parameter $b$, which determines blocks $P_1, \ldots, P_{\lceil N/b\rceil}$ and $Q_1, \ldots, Q_{\lceil N/b\rceil}$.
        \item A region $\mathcal{T} \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ that is either (i) a triangle, (ii) a line segment, or (iii) a point.
        \item A partition of $S := [N/b]$ into subsets $S_{\textnormal{Good}}$ and $S_{\textnormal{Bad}}$, with the requirement $\vert S_{\textnormal{Bad}}\vert \leq \mu$.
        \item For every $i \in S_{\textnormal{Good}}$, access in time $O(1)$ to a constant-complexity quadratic function $f_i$ such that $f_i(t) = \textnormal{Area}((P_i+t)\cap Q_i)$ for all $t \in \mathcal{T}$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

Throughout, we assume that $S_{\textnormal{Good}}$ and $S_{\textnormal{Bad}}$ are sorted, since we can apply radix sort in time $O(\vert S_{\textnormal{Good}}\vert + \vert S_{\textnormal{Bad}}\vert)$. %As stated earlier, our goal in maintaining this data structure is to reduce the time required to find the maximum area translation. To give some intuition, we elaborate on the case that the region $\mathcal{T}$ is a triangle.
When using the block structure, we aim to \emph{decrease} the size of $\mathcal{T}$ while \emph{increasing} the block size $b$. If this is done carefully enough, we get a surprising result: a sublinear time oracle to report $\textnormal{Area}((P+t)\cap Q)$ for any translation $t \in \mathcal{T}$.








\section{The Linear Time Algorithm}
We will reduce Problem \ref{prob:overlap} to a few different subroutines/oracles. Roughly speaking, these allow us to produce a highly refined block structure in linear time, which can be used along with multidimensional parametric search \cite{Toledo92} to find the translation of $P$ maximizing its area of intersection with $Q$ in time $O(N/\log N)$.
\begin{problem}[\textsc{MaxRegion}]
    Given a $(\mu, b, \mathcal{T})$-block structure for $P$ and $Q$, find \[\max_{t \in \mathcal{T}} \textnormal{Area}((P+t)\cap Q)\] along with the translation $t^* \in \mathbb{R}^2$ realizing this maximum.\footnote{During some calls to \textsc{MaxRegion}, $\mathcal{T}$ may \emph{not} contain the global optimum.}
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[\textsc{Cascade}]
    Given a $(\mu, b, \mathcal{T})$-block structure, parameters $\mu'$ and $b'$ such that $b$ divides $b'$, and a triangle or line segment $\mathcal{T}' \subseteq \mathcal{T}$, either produce a $(\mu', b', \mathcal{T}')$-block structure, or report failure.
    Failure may be reported only if the interior of $\mathcal{T}'$ intersects more than $\mu'/2$ lines from the set
    \begin{equation} \label{eq:setS}
        \mathcal{S}\ := \bigcup_{k \in \{0, \ldots, \lceil N/b'\rceil-1\}} \left(\bigcup_{(i, j) \in [\frac{b'}{b}] \times [\frac{b'}{b}]}\overleftrightarrow{\Pi}(P_{k\cdot\frac{b'}{b} + i}, Q_{k\cdot\frac{b'}{b} + j})\right),
    \end{equation}
    where $P_1, \ldots, P_{\lceil N/b\rceil}$ and $Q_1, \ldots, Q_{\lceil N/b\rceil}$ are the blocks of $P$ and $Q$ determined by $b$. %, and $P_1', \ldots, P_{\lceil N/b'\rceil}'$ and $Q_1', \ldots, Q_{\lceil N/b'\rceil}'$ are those determined by $b'$.
\end{problem}

The set $\mathcal{S}$ may appear mysterious, but it simply corresponds to the line extensions for the edges that could ``cause trouble'' for the cascading procedure. The only observation necessary right now is that $\vert \mathcal{S} \vert = O(Nb')$, i.e., $\mathcal{S}$ is near-linear in size for all $b' = N^{o(1)}$.

We write the expected time complexity of the fastest algorithm for \textsc{MaxRegion} as $T_{d\textsc{-Max}}(N, b, \mu)$, where $d$ is the dimension of the region $\mathcal{T}$. We write the expected time complexity of the fastest algorithm for \textsc{Cascade} as $T_{\textsc{Cascade}}(N, b, b', \mu)$. The latter will not depend on $\mu'$ or the dimensions of $\mathcal{T}$ and $\mathcal{T}'$.


The next lemmas will be proved in Sections \ref{sec:t0} and \ref{sec:cascade}. The main idea behind proving each is to group most of the blocks of $P$ and $Q$ into larger convex polygons that intersect in at most a constant number of locations, and then to process the rest via brute force.


%\begin{lemma}[Point Oracle] 
\begin{restatable}[Point Oracle]{lemma}{PointOracleLemma}
\label{lem:T0}
    For all $2 \leq b \leq N$, for all $\mu$, %we have:
    \[T_{0\textsc{-Max}}(N, b, \mu) = O\left(N \cdot \frac{\log^2 b}{b} + \mu b\right).\]
\end{restatable}
%\end{lemma}

%\begin{lemma}[Cascading Subroutine] 
\begin{restatable}[Cascading Subroutine]{lemma}{CascadingLemma}
\label{lem:Cascade}
    For all $2 \leq b, b' \leq N$ such that $b$ divides $b'$, for all $\mu$, %we have:
    \[T_{\textsc{Cascade}}(N, b, b', \mu) = O\left(N \cdot \frac{\log b'\log b}{b} + \mu b^2\right).\]
\end{restatable}
%\end{lemma}

\subsection{Reducing to the Point Oracle and Cascading Subroutine}
We now give an algorithm for \textsc{MaxRegion} when the input $(\mu, b, \mathcal{T})$-block structure has $\mathcal{T}$ being a triangle or line segment. The algorithm works by invoking \textsc{MaxRegion} on regions with lower dimension to iteratively shrink $\mathcal{T}$.

\begin{lemma} \label{lem:subroutineT12}
    For all $2 \leq b, b' \leq N^{o(1)}$ such that $b$ divides $b'$, for all $\mu, \mu'$ such that $\mu' = N^{1-o(1)}$, %we have:
    \begin{align*}
        T_{2\textsc{-Max}}(N, b, \mu)\ =\ \ & O(\log (Nb'/\mu')) \cdot T_{1\textsc{-Max}}(N, b, \mu) + O(N^{0.1 + o(1)}) + {} \\
        & O(1) \cdot T_{\textsc{Cascade}}(N, b, b', \mu) + T_{2\textsc{-Max}}(N, b', \mu')\\[4pt]
    %\end{align*}
    %\begin{align*}
        T_{1\textsc{-Max}}(N, b, \mu)\ =\ \ & O(\log (Nb'/\mu')) \cdot T_{0\textsc{-Max}}(N, b, \mu) + O(N^{0.1 + o(1)}) + {}\\
        & O(1) \cdot T_{\textsc{Cascade}}(N, b, b', \mu) + T_{1\textsc{-Max}}(N, b', \mu').
    \end{align*}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    The algorithm for when $\mathcal{T}$ is a line segment is nearly identical to the algorithm for when $\mathcal{T}$ is a triangle, so for brevity we only focus on the triangle case. Let $P_1, \ldots, P_{\lceil N/b\rceil}$ and $Q_1, \ldots, Q_{\lceil N/b\rceil}$ be the blocks of $P$ and $Q$ determined by block size parameter $b$.

    Define $\mathcal{T}_0 := \mathcal{T}$. We will produce a series of triangles $\mathcal{T}_0 \supset \mathcal{T}_1 \supset \mathcal{T}_2 \supset \ldots$ such that each triangle is guaranteed to contain the optimal translation $t^*$ for the original region $\mathcal{T}$, and then take the final triangle in this sequence as $\mathcal{T}'$. Once we have $\mathcal{T}'$, we invoke \textsc{Cascade} to produce a $(\mu', b', \mathcal{T}')$-block structure, and then find the optimal translation within $\mathcal{T}'$ (along with the area for this translation) by invoking \textsc{MaxRegion} on this new block structure.

    In the subsequent analysis we show that, with high probability, the interior of $\mathcal{T}'$ produced as per our sequence will intersect at most $\mu'/2$ lines of the set $\mathcal{S}$.
    If this event occurs, then \textsc{Cascade} will be \emph{forced} to produce a $(\mu', b', \mathcal{T}')$-block structure. If this event does not occur, and \textsc{Cascade} returns failure, we can re-run the randomized algorithm to produce a new $\mathcal{T}'$ and try again. The expected number of attempts is $O(1)$, and so all of the steps (except for producing $\mathcal{T}'$) have an expected time overhead of $O(1) \cdot T_{\textsc{Cascade}}(N, b, b', \mu) + T_{2\textsc{-Max}}(N, b', \mu')$.  

\begin{figure}
    \centering    \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{Figures/Tseries.png}
    \caption{A series of triangles $\mathcal{T}_0$, $\mathcal{T}_1$, and $\mathcal{T}_2$ as produced by the algorithm. The (unknown) optimal placement $t^*$ is denoted by an asterisk. The red curves indicate contour lines of the objective function, with darker circles indicating larger values of $\textnormal{Area}((P+t) \cap Q)$.}
    \label{fig:Tseries}
\end{figure}

    \paragraph{Producing $\mathcal{T}'$.} As per the above, the expected number of times we need to make $\mathcal{T}'$ is $O(1)$, so we only focus on the time complexity to make $\mathcal{T}'$ once. Consider producing a triangle $\mathcal{T}_{x+1}$ from a triangle $\mathcal{T}_x$ in the sequence. As a first step, we use Lemma \ref{cor:approxcut} to make, with high probability, a $\frac{1}{2}$-cutting for the lines of $\mathcal{S}$ that intersect the interior of $\mathcal{T}_x$.

    To apply this lemma, we need an efficient sampler. We argue that naive rejection sampling will suffice.\footnote{In particular, we start by sampling a random $k \in \{0, \ldots, \lceil N/b'\rceil-1\}$ and a random pair $(i, j) \in [\frac{b'}{b}] \times [\frac{b'}{b}]$. Then we sample a random line from $\overleftrightarrow{\Pi}(P_{k\cdot\frac{b'}{b} + i}, Q_{k\cdot\frac{b'}{b} + j})$ and compute its intersection with $\mathcal{T}_x$. If it intersects the interior, we keep the line; otherwise we discard it and try again.} Observe that if rejection sampling fails to find a random line of $\mathcal{S}$ that intersects the interior of $\mathcal{T}_x$ within time $O(N^{0.1})$, then with high probability only $N^{1-\Omega(1)}$ lines of $\mathcal{S}$ intersect $\mathcal{T}_x$. By assumption $\mu'/2 = N^{1 - o(1)}$, so we can directly take $\mathcal{T}' = \mathcal{T}_x$. If rejection sampling is fast enough, then we can produce the required $\frac{1}{2}$-cutting with high probability in time $O(\vert \mathcal{S}\vert^{0.1+o(1)}) = O(N^{0.1+o(1)})$. Then, we intersect this cutting with $\mathcal{T}_x$ and re-triangulate the resulting cells, which takes $O(1)$ time in total.

    The final step is to locate the cell of this triangulation that contains $t^*$. This cell will be $\mathcal{T}_{x+1}$. To do this, we check all $O(1)$ triangles by recursing in the dimension. We assume that $t^*$ is strictly contained within some cell, as the case that it is on the boundary can be detected similarly. Consider a single triangle $\mathcal{X}$. Let $\mathcal{X}^-$ be an infinitesimally smaller copy of $\mathcal{X}$ that is strictly inscribed within $\mathcal{X}$.\footnote{Infinitesimal shifts are a standard trick, and all of the algorithms in this paper can handle them without modification as long as they are implemented carefully enough.} All $O(1)$ bounding line segments for $\mathcal{X}$ and $\mathcal{X}^-$ are contained within $\mathcal{T}$. Therefore, we can use the input $(\mu, b, \mathcal{T})$-block structure as a block structure for these line segments, and find the optimal translation (along with the area of intersection) restricted to each line segment in time $O(1) \cdot T_{1\textsc{-Max}}(N, b, \mu)$. If one of the line segments for $\mathcal{X}^-$ has a translation realizing a larger area than all of the line segments for $\mathcal{X}$, then by Lemma \ref{lem:concave} we know that $t^* \in \mathcal{X}$. Otherwise, $\mathcal{X}$ does not contain $t^*$.\footnote{All invocations might return a maximum area of zero, but we can handle this in $O(1)$ time by checking whether an arbitrary translation that causes $P$ and $Q$ to overlap is located within $\mathcal{X}$.} By definition of the $\frac{1}{2}$-cutting, the interior of $\mathcal{T}_{x+1}$ will intersect at most half as many lines of $\mathcal{S}$ as $\mathcal{T}_x$.

    By construction and the fact that $b$ divides $b'$ (and hence $b \leq b'$), we have $\vert \mathcal{S}\vert = O(Nb')$. Thus there are at most $O(\log \frac{\vert \mathcal{S}\vert}{\mu'}) = O(\log (Nb'/\mu'))$ iterations before we produce a triangle that intersects at most $\mu'/2$ lines of $\mathcal{S}$ with high probability and hence can be taken as $\mathcal{T}'$. The time for all iterations is $O(\log (Nb'/\mu')) \cdot (T_{1\textsc{-Max}}(N, b, \mu) + O(N^{0.1 + o(1)})) = O(\log (Nb'/\mu')) \cdot T_{1\textsc{-Max}}(N, b, \mu) + O(N^{0.1 + o(1)})$.
\end{proof}



\subsection{Putting it all Together}
We now present our linear time algorithm. This amounts to solving a curious recurrence, and we actually have significant freedom in choosing the values for $b$ and $b'$.

\begin{theorem}
    There is an algorithm for Problem \ref{prob:overlap} running in expected time $O(n+m)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
We will always take $\mu := 20 \cdot N/b^3$ and $\mu' := 20 \cdot N/(b')^3$, so we can drop the $\mu$ and $\mu'$ arguments from the time complexity of each subroutine/oracle. We begin by reducing from Problem~\ref{prob:overlap} to an instance of \textsc{MaxRegion} over a $(20 \cdot N/2^3, 2, \mathcal{T})$-block structure, which requires no work beyond computing the area prefix sums because we can take $S_{\textnormal{Bad}} = [\lceil N/2\rceil]$ and $S_{\textnormal{Good}} = \emptyset$. Thus the total expected running time for Problem \ref{prob:overlap} will be $O(N) + T_{2\textsc{-Max}}(N, 2)$.

Before analyzing $T_{2\textsc{-Max}}(N, 2)$, we find a closed-form expression for $T_{1\textsc{-Max}}(N, b)$ when $2 \leq b \leq N^{o(1)}$. As per Lemmas \ref{lem:T0}, \ref{lem:Cascade}, and \ref{lem:subroutineT12}, we have the following for any $2 \leq b, b' \leq N^{o(1)}$ such that $b$ divides $b'$:
\[T_{1\textsc{-Max}}(N, b) \:=\: O\left(N \cdot \frac{\log b'\log^2 b}{b}\right) + T_{1\textsc{-Max}}(N, b').\]
We can efficiently parallelize the algorithm for zero-dimensional \textsc{MaxRegion} given in the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:T0}. So via a standard application of multidimensional parametric search (see Theorem 4.4 in the full version of \cite{Toledo92}) that uses zero-dimensional \textsc{MaxRegion} for the decision problem, $T_{1\textsc{-Max}}(N, b) = O(N/\log N)$
when $b \geq (\log N)^{c_1}$ for some constant $c_1$. If we take $b' = 2b$ (for example) and solve the recurrence, we get
%\todo{TC: I've changed $b'=b^2$ to $b'=2b$ (here and below) to be consistent with your sum}
\[T_{1\textsc{-Max}}(N, b) \:=\: O\left(\frac{N}{\log N} \ + \sum_{a = \log b}^{O(\log \log N)} N \cdot\frac{\log^3 (2^a)}{2^a}\right) \:=\: O\left(N \cdot \frac{\log^3 b}{b} + \frac{N}{\log N}\right).\]

Now we turn our attention to $T_{2\textsc{-Max}}(N, b)$. Using the above along with Lemmas \ref{lem:T0}, \ref{lem:Cascade}, and \ref{lem:subroutineT12}, we have the following for any $2 \leq b, b' \leq N^{o(1)}$ such that $b$ divides $b'$:
\[T_{2\textsc{-Max}}(N, b) \:=\: O\left(N \cdot \frac{\log b'\log^3 b}{b} + N \cdot \frac{\log b'}{\log N}\right) + T_{2\textsc{-Max}}(N, b').\]
If we again take $b' = 2b$ and stop to apply multidimensional parametric search when $b' = (\log N)^{c_2}$ for some constant $c_2$, we have $O(\log\log N)$ recursion depth, and $\log b'$ never exceeds $O(\log\log N)$. 
Solving the recurrence in the same way as for $T_{1\textsc{-Max}}(N, b)$ gives a loose upper bound of:
\[T_{2\textsc{-Max}}(N, b) \:=\: O\left(N \cdot \frac{\log^4 b}{b} + N \cdot \frac{\log^2\log N}{\log N}\right).\]
Clearly $T_2(N, 2) = O(N)$, and the theorem follows.
\end{proof}

\section{Constructing the Point Oracle} \label{sec:t0}
In this section, we prove the Point Oracle Lemma, restated below:

\PointOracleLemma*

%\noindent
%\textbf{Lemma \ref{lem:T0}} (Point Oracle, Restated).
%    \emph{For all $2 \leq b \leq N$, for all $\mu$,
%    \[T_{0\textsc{-Max}}(N, b, \mu) = O\left(N \cdot \frac{\log^2 b}{b} + \mu b\right).\]}

In other words, we need to use a given $(\mu, b, \mathcal{T})$-block structure (where $\mathcal{T}$ is just a single point $t^*$) to calculate $\textnormal{Area}((P+t^*) \cap Q)$. We can decompose the area function as
\[\textnormal{Area}((P+t^*)\cap Q) \:=\: \sum_{i} \textnormal{Area}((P_i+t^*)\cap Q_i) + \sum_{i \neq j} \textnormal{Area}((P_i+t^*)\cap Q_j)\]
and then solve for each individual summation rapidly. The first summation is quite easy to compute by simply reading through the lists $S_{\textnormal{Good}}$ and $S_{\textnormal{Bad}}$ that are provided.

\begin{lemma}
    There is an algorithm running in time $O(N/b + \mu b)$ such that, given a $(\mu, b, \mathcal{T})$-block structure, where $2 \leq b \leq N^{o(1)}$ and $\mathcal{T}$ consists of just a single point $t^*$, it will compute
    \[\sum_{i} \textnormal{Area}((P_i+t^*)\cap Q_i).\]
    Here $P_1, \ldots, P_{\lceil N/b\rceil}$ and $Q_1, \ldots, Q_{\lceil N/b\rceil}$ are the blocks of $P$ and $Q$ determined by parameter~$b$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    By definition of the block structure, we have a partition of $S = [N/b]$ into two subsets $S_{\textnormal{Good}}$ and $S_{\textnormal{Bad}}$ such that $\vert S_{\textnormal{Bad}}\vert \leq \mu$, and for each $i \in S_{\textnormal{Good}}$ we have a constant complexity quadratic function $f_i(t) = \textnormal{Area}((P_i + t) \cap Q_i)$ for all $t \in \mathcal{T}$.

    We can thus compute
    \[\sum_{i \in S_{\textnormal{Good}}}\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t^*) \cap Q_i)\]
    in time $O(N/b)$ by simply evaluating each $f_i(t^*)$. Since $\vert S_{\textnormal{Bad}}\vert \leq \mu$, we can compute
    \[\sum_{i \in S_{\textnormal{Bad}}}\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t^*) \cap Q_i)\]
    by evaluating the area for each $(P_i + t^*) \cap Q_i$ directly. The time required is $O(\mu b)$ using known algorithms for intersecting two convex polygons whose edges are in sorted order (one such algorithm is given in \cite{o1982new}).
\end{proof}

We now turn our attention to the summation $\sum_{i \neq j} \textnormal{Area}((P_i+t^*)\cap Q_j)$. Because every term amounts to computing the intersection for two blocks with \emph{different} angle ranges, we could use binary searches to evaluate each term in $O(\mathop{\rm polylog}(b))$ time. However, this is too slow for our purposes, as there are a near quadratic number of terms. Instead, we take advantage of additional structure to decompose the summation into $O(N/b)$ intersections of \emph{unions of blocks}, such that each intersection can still be computed via binary search.

As a tool, we need a way to efficiently intersect convex chains that meet certain conditions.

\begin{lemma} \label{lem:layer}
    Let $A$ and $B$ be any two convex polygons, and let $X$ and $Y$ be any two polygonal chains whose edges are a subset of the edges of $A$ and $B$, respectively. If $\Lambda_A(X) \cap \Lambda_B(Y) = \emptyset$, then $X$ and $Y$ intersect at most twice, and we can find the intersection point(s) in time $O(\log \vert X\vert \log \vert Y\vert)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} 
%\todo{Add figure.}
    Because the angle ranges for $X$ and $Y$ are disjoint, they act as pseudo-disks, and hence  can intersect at most twice.
    %We now describe how to find the intersection point(s), if they exist.
    We can locate a vertex of $X$ with respect to the convex chain $Y$ in time $O(\log \vert Y\vert)$ via binary search. Using this as a subroutine, we then binary search on the vertices of $X$ to find the two pairs of consecutive vertices on $X$ that define $X$'s intersections with $Y$. After applying a symmetric process to find two pairs of consecutive vertices on $Y$, we are done. The total time is $O(\log \vert X\vert \log \vert Y\vert)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
    One could apply an algorithm of
    Overmars and van Leeuwen~\cite{OvermarsL81} in the dual to obtain an improved time bound of $O(\log\vert X\vert +\log\vert Y\vert)$, though we don't need this improvement.
\end{remark}

Now we can show how to compute $\sum_{i \neq j} \textnormal{Area}((P_i+t^*)\cap Q_j)$.

\begin{lemma}
    There is an algorithm running in time $O(N \cdot \frac{\log^2 b}{b})$ such that, given a $(\mu, b, \mathcal{T})$-block structure, where $\mathcal{T}$ consists of just a single point $t^*$, it will compute
    \[\sum_{i \neq j}\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t^*) \cap Q_j).\]
    Here $P_1, \ldots, P_{\lceil N/b\rceil}$ and $Q_1, \ldots, Q_{\lceil N/b\rceil}$ are the blocks of $P$ and $Q$ determined by parameter~$b$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    We give a recursive algorithm for the problem. As a subroutine, we consider the problem of computing, given two integers $x, y \in [N/b]$ with $y > x$,
\[
        \sum_{(i, j) \in W(x, y)}\!\!\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t^*) \cap Q_j), 
        \textnormal{ with}\ W(x, y) := \{(i, j) \in [N/b] \times [N/b]: i \neq j,\:  x \leq i, j \leq y\}.
\]   
%    \begin{align*}
%        & \sum_{(i, j) \in W(x, y)}\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t^*) \cap Q_j), \\
%        & 
%        \textnormal{ where}\ W(x, y) := \{(i, j) \in [N/b] \times [N/b]: i \neq j \textnormal{ and } x \leq i, j \leq y\}.
%    \end{align*}
    Let $T(y-x, b)$ be the time complexity of this subroutine. Since the original summation $\sum_{i \neq j}\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t^*) \cap Q_j)$ is equivalent to $\sum_{(i, j) \in W(1, \lceil N/b\rceil)}\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t) \cap Q_j)$, the lemma amounts to showing that $T(\lceil N/b\rceil - 1, b) = O(N \cdot \frac{\log^2 b}{b})$, or more generally, $T(z, b) = O(z\log^2 b)$.

    Consider an instance of this subroutine with any parameters $y > x$. Let $m := \lceil \frac{x+y}{2}\rceil$ and $z := y-x$. We can decompose the target summation as follows:
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{(i, j) \in W(x, y)}&\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t^*) \cap Q_j)\ = \\
        & \sum_{(i, j) \in W(x, m-1)}\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t^*) \cap Q_j) \:+ \sum_{(i, j) \in W(m, y)}\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t^*) \cap Q_j) + {}\\
        & \sum_{x \leq i < m \leq j \leq y}\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t^*) \cap Q_j) \:+ \sum_{x \leq j < m \leq i \leq y}\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t^*) \cap Q_j).
    \end{align*}

    The first two summations can be evaluated in time at most $2 T(\lceil \frac{y-x}{2}\rceil, b)$ via recursion (unless $x-y = 1$, in which case they are automatically zero). The last two summations can actually be evaluated directly. We consider the case of the very last summation; the remaining one can be evaluated via a symmetric process. First note that
    \[\sum_{x \leq j < m \leq i \leq y}\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t^*) \cap Q_j) \:=\: \textnormal{Area}\left(\left(\bigcup_{m \leq i \leq y}P_i+t^*\right) \cap \left(\bigcup_{x \leq j < m}Q_j\right)\right).\]

    Let $A = \bigcup_{m \leq i < y}P_i$ and $B = \bigcup_{x \leq j < m}Q_j$. Observe that $A$ and $B$ are both convex polygons, since each is the union of consecutive blocks. By construction, the edges that $A$ shares with $P$ and the edges that $B$ shares with $Q$ form two convex polygonal chains that have disjoint angle ranges, i.e., $\Lambda_P(E_P(A)) \cap \Lambda_Q(E_Q(B)) = \emptyset$. These chains account for all but two edges of $A$ and two edges of $B$. Thus we can decompose $A$ and $B$ into $O(1)$ convex polygonal chains satisfying the preconditions of Lemma \ref{lem:layer}, which implies that $A$ and $B$ intersect in at most a constant number of locations, and furthermore we can find the intersection points in time $O(\log^2 (bz))$.

    %Using the area prefix sums provided in the block structure and known techniques\todo{TC: what do you mean?} for intersecting convex polygons,
    By decomposing $A$ and $B$ into $O(1)$ pieces based on the intersection locations, we can use the area prefix sums\footnote{The area prefix sums for $P$ and $Q$ also act as ares prefix sums for $A$ and $B$.}, along with a direct computation on the $O(1)$ intersecting pairs of edges, to find the area of intersection in $O(1)$ additional time. % (the area prefix sums for $P$ and $Q$ also act as area prefix sums for $A$ and $B$).
    So the recurrence is $T(z, b) = 2 T(\lceil z/2\rceil, b) + O(\log^2 (bz))$ when $z > 1$ and $T(1, b) = O(\log^2 b)$ otherwise. This solves to $T(z, b) = O(z \log^2 b)$.
\end{proof}

\section{An Algorithm for the Cascading Subroutine} \label{sec:cascade}

The last step is to prove the Cascading Subroutine Lemma, restated below:
%give an algorithm for the cascading subroutine.

\CascadingLemma*

%\noindent
%\textbf{Lemma \ref{lem:Cascade}} (Cascading Subroutine, Restated).
%    \emph{For all $2 \leq b, b' \leq N$ such that $b$ divides $b'$, for all $\mu$, we have:
%    \[T_{\textsc{Cascade}}(N, b, b', \mu) = O(N \cdot \frac{\log b'\log b}{b} + \mu b^2).\]}

Recall that given a $(\mu, b, \mathcal{T})$-block structure, parameters $\mu'$ and $b'$, and a triangle or line segment $\mathcal{T}' \subseteq \mathcal{T}$, our goal is to either produce a valid $(\mu', b', \mathcal{T}')$-block structure, or report that more than $\mu'/2$ lines of the set $\mathcal{S}$ intersect $\mathcal{T}'$ (which is the ``failure'' condition). Throughout, let $P_1, \ldots, P_{\lceil N/b\rceil}$ and $Q_1, \ldots, Q_{\lceil N/b\rceil}$ be the blocks of $P$ and $Q$ determined by block size parameter $b$, and let $P_1', \ldots, P_{\lceil N/b'\rceil}'$ and $Q_1', \ldots, Q_{\lceil N/b'\rceil}'$ be the blocks of $P$ and $Q$ determined by block size parameter $b'$.

First, we show that constant-complexity quadratic functions may be used to summarize the area function for certain block pairs. This observation is already well-known \cite{BergCDKT98}, but we give its proof for the sake of completeness.

\begin{lemma} \label{lem:findquadratic}
    Let $A$ and $B$ be any convex polygons, and let $\mathcal{T}'$ be any triangle or line segment contained in one cell of $\Pi(A, B)$.\footnote{It is permissible for $\mathcal{T}'$ to touch the boundary of one cell, as long as it doesn't strictly intersect.} Then there exists a constant-complexity quadratic function $f$ such that $f(t) = \textnormal{Area}((A+t) \cap B)$ for all $t \in \mathcal{T}'$. 
    %\todo{Can cite [20] and remove the proof, which gives more space for exposition etc.}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By definition of $\Pi(A, B)$ (see Equation \ref{eq:Pi}), if $\mathcal{T}'$ is contained within a single cell, then every translation $t \in \mathcal{T}'$ has the same pairs of edges from $A$ and $B$ intersecting when $A+t$ is intersected with $B$. The gradient for the function $\textnormal{Area}((A+t) \cap B)$, when $t$ is restricted to $\mathcal{T}'$, is precisely the sum of gradients resulting from each of these edge pairs. Each individual gradient is a linear function in $t$, so the overall gradient is as well. Integrating this gives a function $f(t) = \textnormal{Area}((A+t) \cap B)$ that is quadratic in $t$.
\end{proof}

During cascading, we need a way to determine for which indices $i$ we can apply Lemma \ref{lem:findquadratic} to summarize $\textnormal{Area}((P_i'+t) \cap Q_i')$. The lemma below will be used as a tool to achieve this goal.

\begin{lemma} \label{lem:meshintersect}
    Let $A$ and $B$ be any two convex polygons, and let $X$ and $Y$ be any two polygonal chains whose edges are a subset of the edges of $A$ and $B$, respectively. Let $\mathcal{T}'$ be any triangle or line segment. If $\Lambda_A(X) \cap \Lambda_B(Y) = \emptyset$, then we can determine whether there exists a line segment in $\Pi({X}, {Y})$ that intersects the interior of $\mathcal{T}'$, in time $O(\log \vert X\vert \log \vert Y\vert)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    Let the set of delimiting points for $\mathcal{T}'$ be $V(\mathcal{T}')$. For each $t \in V(\mathcal{T}')$, we know by Lemma~\ref{lem:layer} that $X+t$ and $Y$ can intersect in at most two points, and we can find the two pairs of edges that contain these intersection(s) in time $O(\log \vert X\vert \log \vert Y\vert)$.\footnote{There is an edge case where at least one of the intersections occurs at a vertex of $X$ or a vertex of $Y$, but this can be handled with slightly more work without increasing the asymptotic running time.} Denote by $L$ the set of all $O(1)$ edge pairs identified. (If $\mathcal{T}'$ is a triangle, $L$ contains at most six edge pairs; if $\mathcal{T}'$ is a line segment, $L$ contains at most four edge pairs.) We can determine if $\mathcal{T}' \subseteq \pi(e_X, e_Y)$ for all $(e_X, e_Y) \in L$ via brute force in time $O(1)$.  If this is \emph{not} the case, then some line segment of $\Pi(X, Y)$ intersects the interior of $\mathcal{T}'$, and we are done. If this \emph{is} the case, then because $\mathcal{T}'$ is convex and each $\pi(e_{X}, e_Y)$ is convex, every translation $t \in \mathcal{T}'$ has $X+t$ and $Y$ intersecting at every edge pair in $L$.

    There is one more scenario to consider: Some extraneous edge pair $(e_X', e_Y')$ has $\pi(e_X', e_Y')$ intersecting the interior of $\mathcal{T}'$ \emph{without} $\pi(e_X', e_Y')$ containing a point in $V(\mathcal{T}')$, meaning that this new edge pair is not in $L$. Towards identifying this case, let $X^-$ be $X$ with its edges appearing in $L$ removed, and let $Y^-$ be $Y$ with its edges participating in $L$ removed. By construction, for all translations $t \in \mathbb{R}^2$, we have that $X^- + t$ and $Y$ never intersect at an edge pair in $L$, and we have that $X + t$ and $Y^-$ never intersect at an edge pair in $L$. In other words, an extraneous edge pair $(e_X', e_Y')$ exists if and only if at least one of the following holds:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item There exists a translation $t \in \mathcal{T}'$ such that $X^- + t$ strictly intersects $Y$.
        \item There exists a translation $t \in \mathcal{T}'$ such that $X + t$ strictly intersects $Y^-$.
    \end{enumerate}

    We describe how to check for the first case, as the second case is symmetric. First decompose $X^-$ into $O(1)$ contiguous convex polygonal chains $X^-_1, \ldots X^-_{O(1)}$ (these chains are delimited by the edges we removed). For each $X^-_i$, check if the (implicitly generated) Minkowski sum $X^-_i + \mathcal{T}'$ has any intersection with $Y$. While the entire Minkowski sum is not necessarily a convex polygon, its boundary is defined by $O(1)$ convex polygonal chains, and these can be identified in time $O(\log\vert X^-_i\vert)$ via binary searches on $X^-_i$. From here we apply nested binary searches on $Y$ and the bounding polygonal chains, a la Lemma \ref{lem:layer}. This allows us to determine if $X^-_i + \mathcal{T}'$ has any intersection with $Y$ in time $O(\log\vert X^-_i\vert \log\vert Y\vert)$.\footnote{Technically, we also must perform a check to determine if $Y$ is entirely contained within $X^-_i + \mathcal{T}'$, but this can be performed by orienting a single point on $Y$ with respect to the bounding polygonal chains of $X^-_i + \mathcal{T}'$ in time $O(\log\vert X^-_i\vert)$.} After repeating the process for all $O(1)$ choices of $X^-_i$, the total time taken is $O(\log\vert X\vert\log\vert Y\vert)$.
\end{proof}






We are now ready to prove Lemma \ref{lem:Cascade}. The main observation is that the new quadratic functions stored in this data structure only need to apply when $t \in \mathcal{T}'$, not the more general case that $t \in \mathcal{T}$, so many of the previously difficult blocks can now be handled. We will find a quadratic function for almost all indices $k \in [N/b']$ in two steps:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Scan through the information provided in the input $(\mu, b, \mathcal{T})$-block structure, and use this to recover part of the function $\textnormal{Area}((P_k'+t) \cap Q_k')$.
    \item Recover the missing information for $\textnormal{Area}((P_k'+t) \cap Q_k')$ by intersecting $O(\frac{b'}{b})$ pairs of convex polygons with disjoint angle ranges.
    We evaluate these using nested binary searches (Lemma~\ref{lem:layer} or \ref{lem:meshintersect}) and area prefix sums.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{proof}
    For all $k \in [N/b']$, note that because $b$ divides $b'$, we have a set of indices\footnote{The set may be smaller for the very last value of $k$, but this does not impact the proof.} $C_k = \{(k-1)\cdot \frac{b'}{b} + 1, \ldots, k \cdot \frac{b'}{b}\}$ such that $P_k' = \bigcup_{i \in C_k}P_i$ and $Q_k' = \bigcup_{j \in C_k}Q_j$. If for all $(i, j) \in C_k \times C_k$ and for all $t \in \mathcal{T}'$ we can summarize $\textnormal{Area}((P_i+t) \cap Q_j)$ with a constant-complexity quadratic function, then we can simply add these to get a new function $f_k(t) = \textnormal{Area}((P_k'+t) \cap Q_k')$.

    %For every $k$, we want the algorithm to execute in $O(b' \cdot \frac{\log b'\log b}{b})$ time, so that the entire algorithm takes sublinear time $O(\frac{N}{b'} \cdot b' \cdot \frac{\log b'\log b}{b}) = O(N \cdot \frac{\log b'\log b}{b})$. (There will also be an additional global overhead of $O(\mu b^2)$.)
    We first argue that each quadratic function $f_k(t)$ can be found efficiently, if it exists. Consider just a single value of $k$. We use the decomposition
    \[f_k(t)\ = \!\sum_{(i, j) \in C_k \times C_k}\!\!\!\!\textnormal{Area}((P_i+t) \cap Q_j) \:=\: \sum_{i \in C_k}\textnormal{Area}((P_i+t) \cap Q_i)\ + \!\sum_{\substack{(i, j) \in C_k \times C_k:\\ i \neq j}}\!\!\!\!\textnormal{Area}((P_i+t) \cap Q_j).\]

    For the first summation on the right hand side, we scan through the list $S_{\textnormal{Good}}$ from the original $(\mu, b, \mathcal{T})$-block structure. Each function $\textnormal{Area}((P_i+t) \cap Q_i)$ with $i \in S_{\textnormal{Good}}$ already has a quadratic function, and reading these takes time $O(\frac{b'}{b})$ for each $k$, or $O(N/b)$ time for all $k$. Globally, there are at most $\mu$ indices $i$ not in $S_{\textnormal{Good}}$. For each of these, we can check whether $\mathcal{T}'$ lies in a single cell of $\Pi(P_i, Q_i)$ via directly examining each line segment in $\Pi(P_i, Q_i)$ in time $O(b^2)$. If this is the case, then by Lemma \ref{lem:findquadratic}, $\textnormal{Area}((P_i + t) \cap Q_i)$ can be summarized by a quadratic function for all $t \in \mathcal{T}'$, and we find the function via directly examining each parallelogram in $\Pi(P_i, Q_i)$ in time $O(b^2)$; the total additional time is $O(\mu b^2)$.
    (If this fails for some $i\in C_k$, we will add the index $k$ to $S_{\textnormal{Bad}}'$.)

    Now consider the second summation on the right hand side. We can rewrite this as
    \[\sum_{\substack{(i, j) \in C_k \times C_k :\\ i \neq j}}\!\textnormal{Area}((P_i+t) \cap Q_j)\ = \sum_{i \in C_k} \left( \textnormal{Area}((P_i+t) \cap Q_{i, k}^-) + \textnormal{Area}((P_i+t) \cap Q_{i, k}^+) \right),\]
    where $Q_{i, k}^-$ is the union of all $Q_j$'s in the original sum with $j < i$, and $Q_{i, k}^+$ is the union of all $Q_j$'s in the original sum with $j > i$. We evaluate each of the terms individually.

    Consider the case of $\textnormal{Area}((P_i+t) \cap Q_{i, k}^-)$, as the case of $\textnormal{Area}((P_i+t) \cap Q_{i, k}^+)$ is symmetric. Now, $Q_{i, k}^-$ is a convex polygon, because it is the union of consecutive blocks. By construction,  $\Lambda_P(E_P(P_i)) \cap \Lambda_Q(E_Q(Q_{i, k}^-)) = \emptyset$. (In other words, the angle range for the portion of $P_i$ that coincides with $P$ is disjoint from the angle range for the portion of $Q_{i, k}^-$ that coincides with $Q$.) So we can break $P_i+t$ and $Q_{i, k}^-$ into $O(1)$ pieces that satisfy the preconditions of Lemma \ref{lem:layer}, showing that $P_i+t$ and $Q_{i, k}^-$ can only intersect at a constant number of locations for any translation $t \in \mathbb{R}^2$. These same pieces allow us to apply Lemma \ref{lem:meshintersect} to determine if $\mathcal{T}'$ lies in a single cell of $\Pi(P_i, Q_{i, k}^-)$, which takes time $O(\log \vert P_i\vert \log \vert Q_{i, k}^-\vert) = O(\log b\log b')$. If this is the case, then by Lemma \ref{lem:findquadratic} there exists a quadratic function equal to $\textnormal{Area}((P_i+t) \cap Q_{i, k}^-)$ for all $t \in \mathcal{T}'$. The nonconstant components of this quadratic function only depend on the $O(1)$ intersecting pairs of edges, and we have already found these via Lemma \ref{lem:meshintersect}. Thus we can break $P_i$ and $Q_{i, k}^-$ into $O(1)$ pieces based on the intersection points, and use
    %Thus we can use known decomposition techniques for convex polygons,\todo{TC: what do you mean?} along with
    the area prefix sums from the $(\mu, b, \mathcal{T})$-block structure (which also serve as area prefix sums for $P_i$ and $Q_{i, k}^-$), to find the quadratic function equal to $\textnormal{Area}((P_i+t) \cap Q_{i, k}^-)$ in $O(1)$ additional time.
        (If this fails for some $i\in C_k$, we will add the index $k$ to $S_{\textnormal{Bad}}'$.)

    Adding the time across all terms for all $k \in [N/b']$ gives a total running time of $O(N \cdot \frac{\log b\log b'}{b})$.

    Each index $k$ for which we found a function $f_k(t)$ is added to $S_{\textnormal{Good}}'$, and we store its function. As explained previously, the remaining indices are added to $S_{\textnormal{Bad}}'$. The last step is to argue that $\vert S_{\textnormal{Bad}}' \vert \leq \mu'$, meaning we have constructed a valid $(\mu', b', \mathcal{T}')$-block structure.

    By construction, note that every $k \in S_{\textnormal{Bad}}'$ corresponds to an intersection between $\bigcup_{(i, j) \in C_k \times C_k}\Pi(P_{i}, Q_{j})$ and the interior of $\mathcal{T}'$. Also by construction (see Equation \ref{eq:setS}), each line $\overleftrightarrow{\mathcal{L}}$ in $\mathcal{S}$ has at most two indices $k$ such $\overleftrightarrow{\mathcal{L}}$ is the line extension of some line segment in $\bigcup_{(i, j) \in C_k \times C_k}\Pi(P_{i}, Q_{j})$. Therefore we can charge each $k \in S_{\textnormal{Bad}}'$ to different lines of $\mathcal{S}$ in such a way that each line of $\mathcal{S}$ will be charged at most twice. If $\vert S_{\textnormal{Bad}}'\vert > \mu'$, this implies that more than $\mu'/2$ lines of $\mathcal{S}$ intersect the interior of $\mathcal{T}'$, so the algorithm can just return failure in this case. Otherwise, the $(\mu', b', \mathcal{T}')$-block structure is indeed valid.
\end{proof}


























