\section{Limitations and Future Work}

\subsection{Current Limitations}
Despite the theoretical advances, several limitations persist in the proposed framework. The primary constraints include: (1) quadratic time complexity inherent in pairwise user comparisons restricts scalability to very large user bases; (2) cold-start problems endure for new users with limited interaction history due to data sparsity; (3) dynamic preference evolution remains challenging as the framework assumes relative stability of user preferences over time; and (4) privacy constraints emerge from the framework's requirement for sensitive demographic data access \cite{gao2023verifiable} to ensure fairness evaluation.


\subsection{Future Research Directions}

\subsubsection{Causal Inference Integration}
Recent advances in causal reciprocal recommendation show promise for addressing selection bias \cite{yang2024revisiting}. The causal framework models recommendations as bilateral interventions:

\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[Y_{i,j}^{(1,1)}] - \mathbb{E}[Y_{i,j}^{(0,0)}] = \text{ATE}_{i,j}
\end{equation}

Where $Y_{i,j}^{(t_i,t_j)}$ represents the potential outcome when user $i$ receives treatment $t_i$ and user $j$ receives treatment $t_j$ \cite{zhu2023causal}.

\subsubsection{Federated Learning Approaches}
Privacy-preserving federated learning could enable fairness optimization without centralized demographic data access \cite{gao2023verifiable}. The federated objective becomes:

\begin{equation}
\min_{\theta} \sum_{k=1}^K \frac{n_k}{n} \mathcal{L}_k(\theta) + \lambda \mathcal{R}_{fairness}(\theta)
\end{equation}

Where $\mathcal{L}_k$ is the local loss for client $k$ and $\mathcal{R}_{fairness}$ is the fairness regularization term \cite{gao2023verifiable}.

\subsubsection{Large Language Model Integration}
Recent advances in large language models offer opportunities for understanding user preferences through natural language processing of profiles and conversations. This could improve cold-start performance and preference modeling accuracy.

\section{Conclusions}

This comprehensive analysis reveals that dating app recommendation systems face fundamental challenges in balancing accuracy, fairness, and scalability objectives \cite{hutson2018debiasing}. While recent advances in reciprocal recommendation theory provide solid mathematical foundations, significant gaps remain between theoretical frameworks and practical implementation constraints \cite{xia2016reciprocal}.

Current systems achieve modest performance across key metrics, with the best approaches reaching 28.7\% for reciprocal recommendations \cite{yang2024revisiting}. Bias prevalence remains concerning, with popularity bias affecting 78\% of platforms and racial bias impacting 65\% of systems \cite{cho2023popularity}. These findings highlight the urgent need for algorithmic improvements that address fairness concerns while maintaining competitive accuracy.

The proposed FAIR-MATCH framework demonstrates promise for addressing current limitations through enhanced similarity measures, multi-objective optimization, and fairness-aware algorithms \cite{tomita2024fair}. Our empirical validation shows significant improvements in both accuracy and fairness metrics compared to existing approaches. However, successful implementation requires careful consideration of scalability constraints, data sparsity challenges, and ethical implications of algorithmic mediation in romantic relationships \cite{gao2023verifiable}.

Future research should prioritize the development of scalable implementations supporting millions of users, cross-platform bias mitigation strategies, and standardized evaluation metrics for reciprocal recommendation systems \cite{yang2024revisiting}. The field must also address the fundamental tension between algorithmic efficiency and user autonomy, ensuring that technological solutions enhance rather than constrain human romantic connections \cite{hutson2018debiasing}.

The evolution of dating app recommendation systems represents both a technical challenge and a social responsibility, requiring interdisciplinary collaboration to develop solutions that are not only technically sound but also ethically aligned with human relationship values \cite{hutson2018debiasing}. Success in this domain will contribute significantly to the broader understanding of fair and effective recommendation systems across multiple application areas.




