\section{List of stealth corrections}\label{app-stealth}
\newrefsection

\subsection{\textcite{ezzameli2023}}

The phrase ``Regenerate response'' was removed from the article \autocite{cabanac-ezzameli2023}. The first author
confirmed that the phrase was the result of using ChatGPT \autocite{ezzameli-pubpeer2023}. An acknowledgement to this effect was added to the article, and a formal correction was eventually published \autocite{ezzameli2023e}.

\subsection{\textcite{mohamed2023}}

The phrase ``Regenerate response'' was removed from the article \autocite{cabanac-mohamed2023}.
The first author confirmed that the phrase was the result of using AI tools \autocite{mohamed-pubpeer2023}.
Since no declaration of AI usage has been added to the article, it is still in violation of Elsevier policy.

\subsection{\textcite{akhter2023}}

The phrase ``Regenerate response'' was removed from the article between July 27, 2023, and September 19, 2023 \autocite{cabanac-akhter2023}. No formal correction was published.

\subsection{\textcite{rini2023}}

The phrase ``Regenerate response'' was removed from the article \autocite{cabanac-rini2023}.
No formal correction was published,
but as of September 27, 2024, the following statement had been added to the new version of the article:

\excerpt{This article only used AI for proof-reading, while idea and content are the results of author’s research and thought.}

At least one more stealth correction seems to have occurred; as of November 20, 2024, the article contains neither the AI-generated phrase nor the statement concerning AI use.


\subsection{\textcite{velthuizen2023}}

The phrase ``Regenerate response'' was removed from the article. No formal correction was published,
but the following statement was added to the new version of the article:

\excerpt{This article has been republished with minor changes. These changes do not impact the academic content of the article.}

The nature of the correction is not specified, and its impact (or lack thereof) is decided for the reader. Additionally, since there is still no acknowledgement of AI usage in the article, it appears to remain non-compliant with Taylor \& Francis policy. Given the lack of transparency in this approach, I consider it closer to a stealth correction than a formal correction.

