\section{INTRODUCTION}

Cellular networks connect over five billion users worldwide, relying on extensive 3GPP specifications to ensure functionality and reliability~\citep{gsma2025mobile-user-statistics, 3gpp-standard}.
However, these specifications have long been known to suffer from design flaws and underspecification, making them susceptible to security threats such as DoS attacks and user tracking~\citep{hussain2018lteinspector, shaik2017practical-attacks-dos-ref-3-downgrading-ref-1, yang2019hiding-sigover-malicious-message-injection-ref-1}.
Strengthening the reliability of cellular networks requires proactive specification refinement, a continuous process of identifying and treating potential weaknesses to enhance standards~\citep{3gpp.33.855-study-security-aspects-of-5g-networks, 3gpp2024security-assurance}.
While manual weakness analysis~\citep{rupprecht2019breaking-lte-on-layer-2-dns-spoofing-and-website-spoofing-ref-1, hussain2019privacy2019-side-channel, shaik2017practical-attacks-dos-ref-3-downgrading-ref-1} has traditionally been effective, it is increasingly infeasible due to the escalating complexity of cellular systems and the rapid expansion of specification documents\footnote{3GPP standards expanded from 59,258 pages in Release~8 (LTE) to 117,951 pages in Release~15 (5G) and 195,752 pages in Release~18 as of March 2025.}.
Automated approaches, such as formal verification\citep{hussain20195greasoner, klischies2023undefinedbehavior, basin20185g-aka-formal} and NLP~\citep{chen2021bookworm, al2024hermes, rahman2024cellularlint}, offer valuable insights but still require significant expert involvement, lack scalability, and struggle to adapt to evolving specifications.
More critically, they focus primarily on detecting weaknesses, which is only the first step in refinement.
Effective specification refinement extends beyond mere detection—it requires scalable, automated approaches to systematically address and resolve weaknesses within evolving standards.


Recent advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs)~\citep{achiam2023gpt-4-technical-report, GPT4o, dubey2024llama-3-1-meta-llm-advance-ref-3} has led to their remarkable applications, from solving complex computer tasks~\citep{kim2024language-can-solve-computer-tasks-criticize-then-update-prompt} to accelerating scientific discoveries~\citep{zheng2025large-llm-for-scientific-discovery-ref1-nature}.
In this context, we investigate \textit{automated cellular specification refinement}, a previously unthinkable yet now plausible concept with LLMs.
Beyond their emergent abilities~\citep{wei2022emergent}, several key observations suggest they can:
1) Advanced natural language processing abilities~\citep{bubeck2023sparks-of-agi-llm-advance-ref2, hoffmann2022Chinchilla-scaling-law, radford2018gpt1}, strong knowledge retention~\citep{rein2023gpqa, singhal2023large-llm-for-medicine-ref-nature, radford2019gpt2}, and reasoning capabilities~\citep{wei2022cot, kojima2022zero-shot-cot, zelikman2022star} enable interaction with intricate cellular specifications; 
2) Scalability via in-context learning and fine-tuning~\citep{kaplan2020scaling, hoffmann2022Chinchilla-scaling-law, muennighoff2023scaling-data-constrained-llm-training} allows adaptation to evolving standards.
3) Early research has demonstrated LLMs’ potential in related tasks, such as identifying inconsistencies in specifications~\citep{rahman2024cellularlint} and extracting security-relevant clauses~\citep{karim2023spec5g}. 

Despite their potential, refining cellular specifications presents unique challenges, including understanding obscure technical jargon, systematically analyzing lengthy and complex specification fragments to identify or modify weaknesses, and inferring implicit background knowledge not explicitly stated in the context.
These complexities, often beyond those listed, may not be fully captured by LLMs during their general-purpose training.
To understand this gap, we focus on the \textbf{security aspects of cellular specifications} and critically investigate three research questions:
\begin{packeditemize}
\item \textbf{RQ1}: How well can modern LLMs perform the task of refining cellular specifications?
\item \textbf{RQ2}: How can general-purpose LLMs be better adapted to the domain-specific tasks?
\item \textbf{RQ3}: What challenges remain before LLMs can be reliably deployed for refining active specifications?
\end{packeditemize}
\vspace{-0.1em}


We first address the challenge of domain data scarcity, which poses a significant constraint on the application of data-driven LLMs.
Unlike the well-established CVE database for software security, there is currently no widely recognized dataset dedicated to cellular specification refinement.
The GSMA’s CVD program~\citep{gsma2025coordinated-vulnerability-disclosure-programme} hosts only limited known vulnerabilities with minimal details, while disclosed vulnerabilities are typically reported in academic literature case by case.
To address this, we leverage 200,000+ approved Change Requests (CRs) from 3GPP standards, which document specification revisions along with expert comments.
Our key insight is that \textbf{revisions correcting prior clauses inherently reveal weaknesses in earlier specification versions}, tightly aligning with our goal of refining cellular specifications.
We apply LLM-based semantic filters to identify the CRs pertinent to specification weaknesses, especially those security-related ones.
This strategic use of CRs forms the foundation for our answering the research questions.


To systematically evaluate LLMs' domain-specific capabilities (\textbf{RQ1}), we propose \oureval, a principled evaluation framework for cellular specification refinement.
Leveraging CRs, we formalize the abstract specification refinement goal into three LLM-tractable sub-tasks: \fillcrtext (uncovering potential weaknesses in specifications), \outlinerevtext (proposing necessary revisions given weaknesses), and \diffanalysistext (ensuring revisions address identified weaknesses).
Our benchmark comprises 200 high-quality security-related test cases.
Using LLM-as-a-Judge~\citep{zheng2024llm-judge-fastchat}, validated via human studies, we comprehensively evaluate ten open-source LLMs, four proprietary models, and additionally three SOTA reasoning models.
\oureval effectively differentiates the domain-specific abilities of these models.
Even in the hardest yet imperative sub-task, \fillcrtext, the state-of-the-art reasoning model \texttt{GPT-o3-mini} can discover weaknesses in over 127 out of 200 test cases within five trials.
Meanwhile, a meta-analysis of model release timelines further indicates that newer LLMs exhibit progressively stronger domain-specific capabilities.
These findings suggest that LLMs hold promise for automating cellular specification refinement.





To address how general-purpose LLMs can be effectively adapted for refining cellular specifications (\textbf{RQ2}), we explore two approaches: domain-adaptive fine-tuning and prompting.
We highlight our successful fine-tuning of open-source LLMs using a three-stage training recipe—continual pre-training, fine-tuning, and preference enhancement—coupled with our novel rationale augmentation technique.
The resulting domain-specialized 8B LLM achieves remarkable improvements, nearly tripling the performance of its base counterpart (\texttt{LLaMA-3.1-8B}) in the \diffanalysistext task and even surpassing advanced models like \texttt{GPT-4o} and \texttt{DeepSeek-V3} in the \fillcrtext task.
Various experiments are conducted to explore the recipe, including its scalability and extensibility to more advanced base models.
However, we also pinpoint that general-purpose LLMs are not off-the-shelf solutions for refining active cellular specifications.
For \textbf{RQ3}, we study our domain-specialized LLM's detection performance on 30 existing cellular attacks.
Although it successfully identifies all cases, the study reveals key challenges with LLMs in inspecting active specifications, including the need for additional calibration mechanisms to ensure reliability and implicit reliance on the completeness of analyzed clauses.


As the emerging 6G technology integrates additional features~\citep{lin20243gpp-5g-to-6g, gsma2025-6g-study-request}, it inevitably drives the evolution of 3GPP standards and raises security concerns about new specifications.
Our study demonstrates that LLMs present a timely and effective opportunity for automated cellular specification refinement.
Our main contributions are three-fold:
\begin{packeditemize}
    \item \textbf{New insight.} We pioneer LLM adoption for cellular specification refinement and strategically leverage CRs as domain data to form the foundation of a systematic study.
    \item \textbf{Principled domain-specific evaluation.} We establish an evaluation framework, \oureval, which enables the community to understand the domain-specific abilities of modern LLMs. Using \oureval, we conduct an extensive measurement across 16 representative frontier LLMs.
    \item \textbf{Towards domain-specialized LLMs.} We explore avenues for domain specialization, including an effective fine-tuning recipe. We test on known cellular attacks to identify areas for further improvements in steering LLMs.
\end{packeditemize}

