\subsection{Benchmark Establishment}\label{subsec:benchmark}

The use of CRs, which expose real-world specification weaknesses in 3GPP standards, aligns with our requirement for \textbf{certifiable correctness}. 
The semantic categorization operation in~\Cref{subsubsec:data-stage23-eval} ensures adherence to the \textbf{security focus} principle. Examples of the \fillcrtext task are provided in~\Cref{example:fill-cr-1,example:fill-cr-2,example:fill-cr-3}. 
Qualitatively, the test cases in \oureval feature well-structured, focused specification clauses rich in cellular network terminology (e.g., \textit{AUTS}, \textit{VLR/SGS}, and \textit{synchronization failure message}). 
To assess the representativeness of the 200 selected test cases, we conduct a comprehensive structural analysis. 
The benchmark exhibits extensive release and specification coverage, progressive difficulty levels, and long-context complexity. 
Further details and statistical results are in~\Cref{appx:structural-analysis}. 
\oureval serves as a holistic assessment of LLM capabilities, encompassing extensive domain knowledge, systematic reasoning, precise instruction following, effective long-context processing, a deep understanding of cellular specification weaknesses, and acute awareness of security-related vulnerabilities. 





\subsection{Automatic Metric: LLM-as-a-Judge}\label{subsec:LLM-as-a-Judge}

We require an effective metric to evaluate the correctness of LLM responses.
Although we have reference answers derived from CRs, conventional string-matching-based metrics like ROUGE or BLEU have been widely reported to correlate poorly with human evaluation in text generation tasks~\citep{schluter2017limits-of-rouge, reiter2018structured-review-of-limitations-of-bleu, sun2024source-code-summarization-in-the-era-of-llms-llm-as-a-judge-ref}.
Recent research has shown promising results in utilizing GPT models to assess LLM outputs~\citep{zheng2024llm-judge-fastchat, chen2024alpagasus-data-cleaning-ref-1, ye2024justice-or-prejudice-LLM-as-a-Judge-reference-aware-ref}, a paradigm termed LLM-as-a-Judge. 
This paradigm has been successfully applied to security tasks~\citep{fang2024usenix-llm-for-sec-code-analysis-ref-2-LLM-as-a-Judge-sec-task-1, ullah2024llms-for-sec-code-vuln-ref-1}, including evaluating LLMs' reasoning about code vulnerabilities.
We adopt LLM-as-a-Judge to enable an effective \textbf{automatic evaluation}.

Following prior work~\citep{zheng2024llm-judge-fastchat,fang2024usenix-llm-for-sec-code-analysis-ref-2-LLM-as-a-Judge-sec-task-1, ullah2024llms-for-sec-code-vuln-ref-1}, we use a reference-aware, point-wise LLM-as-a-Judge setting, where each LLM-generated answer is scored by comparing it to the reference answer provided at the start of the context.
As the reference answers are typically self-contained, we omit the original questions from the context.
LLM-as-a-Judge evaluates responses using a 5-point Likert scale~\citep{likert1932technique}, where the positive two points indicate acceptance, allowing differentiation between varying degrees of acceptance.
The detailed prompt template is shown in~\Cref{box:evaluate-fill-cr}, with minor task-specific variations.
We instantiate the LLM-as-a-Judge with \texttt{GPT-4o}~\citep{GPT4o}, the most advanced LLM at the time of writing.
For consistent scoring, we prompt LLM-as-a-Judge to directly give back the scoring and greedily decode with temperature as 0.

We validate the reliability of our final LLM-as-a-Judge setup through a human study, involving eight participants, all PhD students majoring in network security.
The study includes two rounds: an alignment test and a judgment approval test.
Detailed settings and results are provided in~\Cref{appx:human-study-details}.
Key findings include:
\begin{packeditemize}
    \item \textbf{Availability:} Manual checking is extremely labor-intensive, underscoring the need for efficient automatic methods. LLM-as-a-Judge is rather fast and accessible.
    \item \textbf{Conformity:} 
    Human participants frequently disagree on accepting LLM responses while LLM-as-a-Judge typically yields agreements with the majority of participants.
    \item \textbf{Reliability:} Although human participants may have distinct judgment criteria, most LLM-as-a-Judge's evaluations are acceptable for them after calibration.
\end{packeditemize}



\subsection{Evaluation Setup}\label{subsec:exp-setup}
\noindent \textbf{Models.}
Our evaluation captures the currently highest achievable performance of LLMs within three categories: 
1) medium-sized LLMs with 7–14 billion parameters, 
2) advanced LLMs, including giant-sized and closed-source models, and 3) SOTA reasoning models.
\Cref{tab:evaluated-models} details the models.


\noindent \textbf{Metrics.} 
We evaluate LLM performance on \oureval using \passk~\citep{chen2021oai-codex-and-humaneval}. 
The \passk metric measures the success rate by allowing $k$ independent attempts and considering the best result among the $k$ completions.
Given $n \geq k$ completions, where $c \leq n$ completions are correct (i.e., accepted by the LLM-as-a-Judge), the unbiased \passk score is computed as:
$\text{pass@}k := 1 - \frac{\binom{n - c}{k}}{\binom{n}{k}}$
Specifically, we report the cumulative \passk score over all test cases, with a maximum of 200.
Following established practices~\citep{roziere2023code-llama, chen2021oai-codex-and-humaneval, gu2024cruxeval-800samples-input-prediction-and-output-prediction}, we set the sampling temperature to 0.8 and top-p to 0.95.
Balancing reliability and cost, we sample $n=10$ completions.


