\subsection{Evaluation Results}\label{subsec:evaluation-results}

\noindent \textbf{General performance.}
As shown in~\Cref{tab:benchmark-results}, the three tasks vary in difficulty, from the easiest \outlinerevtext to the hardest \fillcrtext.
The \outlinerevtext task, primarily a summarization task, is well-handled by most models, with some smaller models (\texttt{Qwen-2.5-7B}, \texttt{GLM-4-9B}) even outperforming closed-source counterparts (e.g., \texttt{GPT-4o}).
In contrast, the \diffanalysistext task reveals a significant gap between medium-sized and advanced LLMs, highlighting the challenge of identifying implicit specification weaknesses even when given structured version comparison.
The \fillcrtext task emerges as an extremely challenging task, with even advanced models like \texttt{GPT-4o} achieving limited success.
Recent reasoning models (\texttt{GPT-o3-mini}, \texttt{Gemini-2.0-flash-thinking}) demonstrate superior performance, particularly in the \diffanalysistext and \fillcrtext tasks. 
This suggests that reasoning-optimized LLMs hold a significant advantage in tasks requiring deep contextual understanding and structured reasoning.

\noindent \textbf{Propagation of LLM improvements to domain tasks.}
A meta-analysis of LLM release timelines and their performance in \oureval (e.g., \passatk{5} scores in \diffanalysistext across progressively released GPT models) reveals a clear trend:
Improvements in general-purpose LLMs indeed transfer to domain-specific tasks. 
This highlights their growing potential in tackling tasks of mobile systems, at least for refining cellular specifications as explored in this study.

\noindent
\textbf{Failures of certain well-recognized LLMs.}
Meanwhile, we also notice that certain widely recognized LLMs, e.g., \texttt{DeepSeek-R1} and \texttt{Claude-3.5-Sonnet}, perform relatively poorly in the \fillcrtext task.
Another intriguing phenomenon is an inverse scaling~\citep{mckenzie2023inverse-scaling} observed in the \texttt{Qwen-2.5} family, where the largest 72B model performs the worst in the \fillcrtext task compared to its smaller-sized cousins. 
These underscores \textit{the potential gap between general-purpose LLMs and domain-specific task requirements} while emphasizing the importance of \oureval in helping practitioners identify models with the strongest domain-specific capabilities.



\noindent \textbf{Domain-specialized LLM.}
While we defer a detailed discussion of our efforts for LLM domain specialization to the next section, we report in~\Cref{tab:benchmark-results} the performance of our domain-specialized LLM, \ourmodel\footnote{We name it \critic, for the model can act as a \textbf{critic} for cellular specifications, and its power can be attributed to \textbf{C}hange \textbf{R}equests.}, which is obtained by fine-tuning \texttt{LLaMA-3.1-8B} on domain datasets.
\ourmodel nearly triples \passatk{5} scores of its base model in the \diffanalysistext and \fillcrtext tasks. 
Notably, it outperforms its \textbf{contemporary} proprietary models like \texttt{GPT-4o}, solving almost twice as many \fillcrtext test cases.
Despite these advancements, we have to acknowledge that state-of-the-art LLMs, particularly the most recent reasoning models, continue to improve at an unprecedented pace.
At the time of writing, the most advanced reasoning models like \texttt{GPT-o3-mini} have surpassed our domain-specialized model, which was trained on earlier, medium-sized foundation models. 
We further demonstrate in~\Cref{subsubsec:scalability} that fine-tuning on domain-specific data can be effectively extended to more powerful foundation models, demonstrating its future utility.
Overall, these results highlight the impact of domain-specific fine-tuning in bridging the gap between general LLM capabilities and domain-specific requirements.



\takeaway{1}{LLMs demonstrate varying degrees of abilities in the specification refinement tasks. Specifically, newer models with stronger general-domain abilities usually lead to stronger domain-specific abilities as well. \oureval enables the practitioners to understand which LLM is better.}




\section{DOMAIN-SPECIALIZED LLMS FOR SPECIFICATION REFINEMENT}
\label{sec:model-training}
We explore strategies to adapt general-purpose LLMs for domain specialization, focusing on two key methods: domain-adaptive fine-tuning and lightweight prompting.



\subsection{Method 1: Fine-Tuning}\label{subsec:fine-tuning}


Building on previous successful practices in developing specialized LLMs in other domains~\citep{wu2023bloomberggpt-llm-for-finance-ref,singhal2023large-llm-for-medicine-ref-nature,azerbayev2024llemma}, we resort to fine-tuning to achieve domain specialization, with its advantages explained in~\Cref{appx:why-fine-tuning}.
We propose a three-stage expertise training framework that mirrors human expert development.
We also propose a necessary data augmentation operation (rationale augmentation in~\Cref{subsec:rationale-augmentation}) for converting CRs into high-quality training data.

\begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figs/overview-rescaled.pdf}
    \captionsetup{skip=-18pt}
    \caption{\label{fig:overview}High-level overview of the training framework for domain specialization.}
    \Description{}
\end{figure*}

\subsubsection{Three-stage training framework}
This framework, as illustrated in~\Cref{fig:overview}, cultivates domain knowledge, problem-solving abilities for refining cellular specifications, and sensitivity to security-related weaknesses.

\noindent \textbf{Stage 1: Domain-Adaptive Continual Training (DACT).}
LLMs typically undergo pre-training on massive text corpora, through which they develop general-purpose capabilities rather than become adept in specific domains.
Domain-Adaptive Continual Training (DACT) refines an LLM’s learned distribution through incremental training on domain data~\citep{gururangan2020donot-stop-pretraining-dapt-concept, zhou2024lima,ghosh2024closer-look-at-ift-limitations}. 
As we cannot assume that foundation models have adequately acquired domain knowledge during their initial pre-training, we incorporate a DACT stage, which shares the same paradigm with pre-training as follows:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{DACT}}(\theta) = -\mathbb{E}_{x \sim \mathcal{D}_{\text{DACT}}} \left[\sum_{i=1}^{n} \log p_\theta(x_i|x_{<i})\right],
\end{equation}
where $x$ is a sequence from the domain dataset $\mathcal{D}_{\text{DACT}}$, $x_i$ is its $i$-th token, $x_{<i}$ represents preceding tokens, and $p_\theta$ is the probability distribution modeled by parameters $\theta$. 
This objective minimizes the expected negative log-likelihood of predicting the next token given the context.
We instantiate $\mathcal{D}_{\text{DACT}}$ with \stageonedata introduced in~\Cref{subsec:dataset-curation}.



\noindent \textbf{Stage 2: Task-Specialized Tuning (TST).}
After the continual pre-training stage, we fine-tune LLMs in domain tasks during the Task-Specialized Tuning (TST) stage.
This stage is designed to help LLM master the basic ability to analyze cellular specifications.
Fine-tuning during this stage utilizes our \stagetwodata dataset, which encompasses all CR data, and relies on labeled samples:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{TST}}(\theta) = -\mathbb{E}_{(x,y) \sim \mathcal{D}_{\text{TST}}}
\left[ \sum_{t=1}^{T_y} \log p_{\theta}(y_t \mid x,y_{<t}) \right],
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{D}_{\text{TST}}$ represents the \stagetwodata dataset.
The model is trained to minimize the conditional negative log-likelihood of the target output $y$ given the input $x$, as determined by its parameterized distribution $p_{\theta}$.
The \stagetwodata dataset incorporates diverse task formulations, enabling the model to learn each CR through multiple contexts.
This multi-task learning paradigm encourages the model to generalize reasoning skills across the three target tasks.

\noindent \textbf{Stage 3: Security-Centric Tuning (SCT).}
We expect the model to analyze specifications from the security perspective.
Inspired by~\citep{he2023sven-ccs-llm-for-sec-code-ref}, we frame security-centric analysis as a style-controlled text generation problem.
This approach leverages security-related CRs, which reveal real-world security issues, to shape security-centric analysis, enhancing operational feasibility.
The objective for the SCT stage is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{SCT}}(\delta\theta) = -\mathbb{E}_{(x,y) \sim \mathcal{D}_{\text{SCT}}} \left[ \sum_{t=1}^{T_y} \log p_{\theta + \delta\theta}(y_t \mid x, y_{<t}) \right],
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{D}_{\text{SCT}}$ denotes security-related task instances of the target task from \stagethreedata.
The parameter $\delta\theta$ corresponds to the additional adapter implemented using Low-Rank Adapter (LoRA)~\citep{hu2022lora}, which allows for parameter-efficient adaptation and preserves most of the model's original capabilities\citep{biderman2024lora-learn-less-and-forget-less}.



\subsubsection{Rationale augmentation}\label{subsec:rationale-augmentation}
Manual inspection reveals a limitation of training samples derived from CRs: expert rationales \reasontext in CRs are mainly concise declarative statements, rather than detailed reasoning (see~\Cref{fig:rationale-augmentation-example} for an example).
This gap hinders effective LLM training, as insufficient rationales lead models to memorize answers instead of developing problem-solving skills~\citep{chung2024scaling-instruction-fine-tuned-llm, kim2023cot-collection-dataset-rationalization-ref-3, yue2024mammoth}.
This impact is pronounced in \oureval tasks (cf.~\Cref{subsubsec:scalability}), where fine-tuning directly on CR-converted data alone degrades performance.

\begin{figure}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figs/example-of-rationale-enrichment.pdf}
    \vspace{-2.5em}
    \caption{\label{fig:rationale-augmentation-example}Example of rationale augmentation.}
    \Description{}
    \vspace{-1.5em}
\end{figure}

To address this issue, we introduce rationale augmentation, generating refined, rationale-rich responses for LLM training.  
Following prior work on training with rationales~\citep{rajani2019explain-yourself-rationalization-ref--2, zelikman2022star, kim2023cot-collection-dataset-rationalization-ref-3}, we adopt a backward-rationalization strategy:  
A rationale generator \( P_{\text{o}} \) processes a complete task instance—comprising task instruction \( T \), test case \( Q \), and original answer \( A \)—and applies backward reasoning to produce a rationale-augmented answer \( A^* \), following augmentation principles \( C \), as formulated by \( A^* \leftarrow P_{\text{o}}(T \mid Q \mid A; C) \). 
We enforce pedagogically oriented principles $C$ to enhance instructional effectiveness while preserving answer consistency (cf.~\Cref{box:enrich-answer-rationale}).  
This improves domain data quality, aligning CR-based training with the high reasoning demands of specification refinement.  
Moreover, rationale augmentation introduces a new scalability dimension: 
Multiple distinct rationales can support the same correct answer.  
By sampling rationale generators with high temperatures, we generate diverse rationale-augmented answers per task instance, maximizing the value of each CR.  




\subsubsection{Implementation}~\label{subsubsec:implementations}



\noindent \textbf{Rationale augmentation.}
We employ \texttt{LLaMA-3.1-70B} and \texttt{GPT-4o} for rationale augmentation of \stagetwodata and \stagethreedata, respectively.
We prompt the rationale generators with a 0.8 temperature to encourage rationale diversity and a top-p of 0.95 to minimize sampling noise.
Our default rationale number per instance is three for TST and five for SCT.
Note that the reference answers on \oureval are not augmented as we wish to align with human experts completely.




\noindent \textbf{Training configurations.}
Constrained by resource limit, our experiments are primarily on {\raggedright \texttt{LLaMA-3.1-8B}}~\citep{dubey2024llama-3-1-meta-llm-advance-ref-3}, the most powerful 8B model at the inception of this project. 
We fine-tune all parameters for DACT and TST while integrating LoRA adapters\citep{hu2022lora} ($r=256$, $\alpha=512$) for SCT.
We provide our exhaustive training choices in~\Cref{appx:exp-setup}, including learning rates and batch sizes.

\noindent \textbf{Compute infrastructure.}
All experiments were conducted on a server running Ubuntu 20.04.5 LTS operating system.
The machine is equipped with an Intel Xeon Platinum 8468V processor (96 cores, 192 threads), 2 TB of system memory, and 8 NVIDIA H800 GPUs with 80 GB of VRAM each.

\noindent \textbf{Software.}
Our project is implemented based on Python 3.12, CUDA 11.8, PyTorch 2.4.0, and HuggingFace's transformer library. 
To accelerate training, we achieve data parallel through DeepSpeed~\citep{rajbhandari2020zero-deepspeed}: we adopt ZeRO stage-2 with a world size of 4 for 8B models and stage-3 with a world size of 8 for 70B models.
We use Flash-Attention 2~\citep{dao2024flashattention-2} to improve throughput and use gradient checkpointing to reduce memory requirements. 
For evaluation, we deploy inference endpoints using vLLM~\citep{kwon2023vllm}. 
The entire project consumes around 12,800 lines of code, decomposed in~\Cref{tab:code-lines}.



