\noindent \textbf{Extensibility to advanced base models.}
We extend the domain-adaptive fine-tuning to closed-sourced \texttt{GPT-4o-mini}\footnote{Fine-tuning GPT models is officially accessible via \url{https://platform.openai.com/finetune}. Considering cost affordability, we select GPT-4o-mini as a representative example of closed-source models.} and the \texttt{LLaMA-3.1-70B} with a larger parameter count, using the \stagethreedata.
As shown in~\Cref{fig:exp-param-scaling}, domain-adaptive fine-tuning yields performance improvements across all models.
Interestingly, \texttt{LLaMA-3.1-8B} and \texttt{LLaMA-3.1-70B} converge to similar levels, indicating that domain data quality, rather than model size, is the primary bottleneck in certain cases.
Meanwhile, \texttt{GPT-4o-mini} consistently maintains superior performance, particularly on the \fillcrtext task.
This suggests that applying our training methodology to advanced foundation models could further specialize LLMs.









\noindent \textbf{Scaling along rationale\footnote{For clarity, we use the term "rationales" as a shorthand to refer to those distinct rationale-augmented answers in the following contents.} dimension.}
Beyond increasing model size and training data, rationale augmentation offers another scaling dimension to enhance performance, enabling better utilization of constrained domain data.
We evaluate this approach using \stagethreedata in the \diffanalysistext task with various rationale generators.
The landscape of scaling up rationales is depicted in~\Cref{fig:exp-rationale-scaling}.
\textbf{Training without rationales degrades performance, confirming that raw task instances offer limited learning value,} underscoring the necessity of rationale augmentation.
Typically, more capable rationale generators bring about more performance improvement, potentially due to better knowledge distillation~\citep{gou2021knowledge-distillation-survey}.
In most cases, the benefits of incorporating more rationales show evident gains and then reach a plateau.
An exception is observed with \texttt{LLaMA-3.1-8B}, where training with highly diverse rationales (e.g., 20) generated by itself leads to model collapse (see~\Cref{appx:explain-rationale} for a preliminary explanation).






We also explore the ability transfer between \oureval tasks (\Cref{appx:task-correlation}), track the behaviors of the domain-specialized model (\Cref{appx:track-behavior}), ablate the three training stages (\Cref{appx:ablation-study}), and scale up model training along the data dimension (\Cref{appx:data-dim-scalability}).

