\begin{packeditemize}
\item {\textbf{Requirements for effective calibration:}}
While LLMs’ ability to produce diverse interpretations can be useful, it inevitably increases false positives. 
Their inherent hallucination issues~\citep{zhang2023siren-song-survey-hallucination-llms} further exacerbate this, making blind reliance infeasible.
For weakness verification, current practices primarily delegate this responsibility to human analysts~\citep{chen2021bookworm, hussain2018lteinspector, hussain20195greasoner}.
Extending this practice to LLM-based analysis would be impractical due to the sheer volume of generated weaknesses.
Crucially, we argue that even with full autonomy, decision-making should not be ceded to LLMs.
Rather than blaming LLMs, the focus should be on developing effective calibration mechanisms to reduce human effort.
Without them, unverified proposals risk overwhelming analysts instead of aiding them.



\item {\textbf{Completeness of analyzed clauses:}} 
Our manually curated set of attack-related specification clauses provides an idealized benchmark, containing sufficient context for analysis.
This partially explains why \ourmodel achieves perfect detection of known attacks despite its limitations in addressing all test cases in \oureval.
However, practical challenges emerge when refining active specifications, particularly in identifying vulnerabilities arising from complex interactions across multiple sources.
\end{packeditemize}



\takeaway{3}{Despite their potential, LLM-driven cellular specification refinement should not be over-relied on. At the current stage, human-in-the-loop oversight is still necessary to validate LLM-provisioned proposals of specification refinements.}




\section{LIMITATIONS AND FUTURE WORKS}

Due to cost constraints, our measurement study does not cover all existing LLMs; instead, we focus on recent state-of-the-art models as representative examples.
Future work may include newly released LLMs.
Our systematic exploration consumed over 32,120 H800 GPU hours, but computational constraints prevented us from exploring other promising directions, e.g., scaling to giant LLMs like \texttt{LLaMA-3.1-405B}.
Nevertheless, our experiments in~\Cref{subsubsec:scalability} demonstrate the feasibility of extending domain specialization to stronger base models.
This work mainly explores fine-tuning and prompting to enhance LLMs' domain-specific capabilities. 
Alternative approaches such as reinforcement learning and agentic AI may further boost domain specialization, which we leave for future work.
Additionally, while our focus is on the security aspects of cellular specifications, our future work could extend LLM-driven specification refinement to address other types of weaknesses following similar methodologies. 



\section{RELATED WORKS}
\label{sec:related-works}

\subsection{Weakness Discovery of Cellular Specs}
Although cellular specification refinement is rarely studied, well-established practices exist for weakness discovery.

\noindent \textbf{Manual inspection} by domain experts has led to numerous vulnerability findings, including data plane attacks~\citep{rupprecht2019breaking-lte-on-layer-2-dns-spoofing-and-website-spoofing-ref-1, rupprecht2020imp4gt, li2015lyj-VoLTE}, attacks against emergency systems~\citep{bitsikas2022you-emergency, lee2019your-president-spearking-attack}, key reinstallation~\citep{raza2021key-reinstallation-in-lte-ref-1}, user tracking~\citep{chlosta20215g-suci-catcher, hong2018guti-reallocation-demystified-user-tracking-ref-1, shaik2017practical-attacks-dos-ref-3-downgrading-ref-1}, side channel attacks~\citep{bae2022watching-the-watchers-video-identification-traffic-fingerprinting-ref-2, hussain2019privacy2019-side-channel}, and fingerprinting~\citep{kohls2019lost-traffic-encryption-traffic-fingerprinting-ref-1, shaik2019exposing-device-capabilities-device-identification-ref-1}.
While it allows for the discovery of sophisticated attacks, relying on manual inspection cannot ensure covering all aspects of cellular networks, especially as cellular networks grow increasingly complicated and specifications become more voluminous.
That's why automated tools are in demand.


\noindent \textbf{Formal verification} is widely used in evaluating cellular protocols~\citep{hussain20195greasoner, basin20185g-aka-formal, akon2023formal-5g-access-control-nrf-hussain, al2024hermes, tu2014control-plane-lyj, hussain2018lteinspector, klischies2023undefinedbehavior}. 
However, their applications require significant manual effort to build formal models and craft verification properties, inherently limiting automation and scalability.
Moreover, a huge semantic gap exists between abstract formal models and natural language specifications, leading to incomplete exploration of complicated cellular systems.
This challenge is further exacerbated by the inherent state explosion problem of formal methods.




\noindent \textbf{NLP} techniques have been adopted to uncover specification flaws~\citep{chen2021bookworm, chen2022creek, chen2023sherlock, rahman2024cellularlint, al2024hermes}.
Atomic~\citep{chen2021bookworm} applies textual entailment to detect risky descriptions in 3GPP standards.
Several approaches fine-tune encoder-based transformer models (e.g., RoBERTa~\citep{liu2019roberta}) for different purposes:
CREEK~\citep{chen2022creek} identifies security-related CRs, CellularLint~\citep{rahman2024cellularlint} detects inconsistencies, and Hermes~\citep{al2024hermes} constructs state machines for formal analysis.
These methods focus on information extraction rather than direct vulnerability detection, still relying on human expertise and facing scalability challenges.

\subsection{LLMs for Cellular Network}
The human-like intelligence of modern LLMs, specifically generative ones such as \texttt{GPT}~\citep{GPT4o}, has catalyzed numerous studies on their potential applications in cellular networks, raising the question of whether they can serve as key enablers of network automation.
Existing research predominantly investigates whether LLMs can comprehend domain knowledge through question-answering tasks.
For example, GSMA has officially launched the Open-Telco LLM Benchmark project~\citep{gsma2025open-telco-llm-benchmarks} to evaluate LLMs on interacting with complex standards.
Similar efforts include SPEC5G~\citep{karim2023spec5g}, TSpec-LLM~\citep{nikbakht2024tspecllmopensourcedatasetllm}, and TeleQnA~\citep{maatouk2023teleqna}.
Beyond knowledge comprehension, preliminary studies explore whether LLMs can facilitate real-world scenarios. 
For instance, \citet{wen20246g-hotnet-llm-explain-data-traffic-ref-1} employ LLMs to detect and explain runtime anomalies in the O-RAN data plane, while \citet{kotaru2023adapting-llm-for-operator-data-analysis} investigate their potential for enabling natural language interfaces to retrieve and analyze 5G operator network data.
In this work, we stress-test LLMs in a productive setting, evaluating their effectiveness in refining cellular specifications—a task that directly impacts the efficiency of practitioners, particularly those working with voluminous cellular specifications.


\vspace{0.5em}

\section{CONCLUSION}\label{sec:conclusion}
Currently, cellular network specification refinement remains labor-intensive and unsystematic.
In this work, we pioneer the adoption of LLMs for automated cellular specification refinement.
To advance it, we tackle the domain data scarcity challenge by transforming change requests of 3GPP standards into utilizable task instances.
We establish a principled evaluation, \oureval, enabling the community to assess the domain-specific capabilities of the rapidly advancing LLMs.
In this context, we enhance LLM domain specialization by contributing an effective domain-adaptive training recipe and understanding the efficacy of various prompting methods.
Our case studies on 30 known cellular attacks reveal the remaining challenges in achieving fully automated LLM-based cellular specification refinement.
This study sheds light on the potential of LLMs in refining cellular specifications and provides a foundation for future advancements.






