\subsection{Hypotheses and statistical analysis}

The collected data were analyzed using a combination of quantitative and qualitative methodologies. The quantitative analysis was conducted using the R statistical software (version 4.4.1), while the qualitative analysis was performed using the NVivo qualitative data analysis software (version 14.24.0).

\subsubsection{Descriptive statistics}

Means and standard deviations (Tables \ref{tab:mean_and_sd}) were calculated for all constructs within the survey to examine differences in participants' perceptions of the selected psychological factors and their user experiences. A pie chart (Fig. \ref{fig:pie}) was plotted to visualize the preferred SAV choices.

\subsubsection{Favorite SAVs}

To investigate the effects of prompts on participants' user experiences with the four types of SAVs, we first tested whether participants' favorite SAVs varied significantly. If all SAVs were equally preferred, it could indicate that the tailored prompts utilized to develop the four SAV groups did not achieve the anticipated effect on users' experiences or choices. This resulted in the formulation of our initial hypothesis:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{H1}: The favorite SAVs are different across all participants.
\end{itemize}

Although the order of SAV presentations was randomized to minimize any ordering effect on participants' perceptions, it is essential to examine whether the presentation order had any influence on their preferences. For instance, as participants engage with the SAV UIs over time, they may develop a heightened sense of psychological ownership due to increased familiarity, which could impact the efficacy of the prompts. In light of the aforementioned considerations, we put forth a second hypothesis:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{H2}: There is a relationship between the SAV order and the favorite SAV.
\end{itemize}

The user study employed three distinct anthropomorphic characteristics for the SAVs, which were as follows: ``cool and sophisticated'', ``friendly and engaging'', and ``sassy and tired''. Participants selected their preferred anthropomorphic style before interacting with the SAVs and indicated their favorite SAV at the end of all interactions. It is important to ascertain whether participants' preferences for particular SAVs were shaped by the tailored prompts or by the SAVs' anthropomorphic attributes. In other words, participants may exhibit a systematic preference for one SAV over others based on their perception of its interaction style. This leads to our third hypothesis, which is as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{H3}: There is an association between the selected anthropomorphism (e.g., friendly or cool) and the favorite SAV.
\end{itemize}

To test these hypotheses, non-parametric tests were used. Specifically, the Chi-Square Test and Fisher’s Exact Test were applied depending on whether the expected frequency for each category was greater or less than 5, respectively.


\subsubsection{Prompt effect}

The effects of the designed prompts were examined by comparing the survey responses collected after each interaction with the SAVs. Cronbach's alpha values were first assessed to verify the internal consistency of the questionnaire items. Next, the Friedman test, a non-parametric alternative to repeated measures ANOVA that is robust to departures from normality \citep{Nonparametric2015}, was conducted to determine whether there were significant differences between the four SAVs. Specifically, the test was applied to psychological ownership (PO), anthropomorphism (A), quality of service (QoS), disclosure tendency (DT), polarity (Pol), subjectivity (Sub), enjoyment of interaction (PE), and behavioral intention to use (BI). Any significant variation in these constructs would indicate that the prompts influenced participants' perceptions and experiences, consistent with the following overarching hypothesis:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{H4}: A significant difference exists among the four SAV groups across the measured variables.
\end{itemize}

To investigate how PO prompts and A prompts influence user perceptions and experiences, a series of one-tailed Wilcoxon Signed-Rank tests were employed at the item level. In addition, Holm–Bonferroni corrections were applied within each family of pairwise comparisons to control the family-wise error \citep{armstrongWhenUseBonferroni2014}.

\bigskip

\textbf{\textit{Psychological ownership prompt effect}}

It was first hypothesized that integrating PO prompts in an SAV interface would enhance users’ sense of ownership towards the SAV. Specifically, it was hypothesized that:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{H5.1}: SAV2 (integrated with PO prompts) > SAV1 (Baseline) on PO items (PO1–PO4).

    \item \textbf{H5.2}: SAV4 (both PO and A prompts) > SAV3 (integrated with A prompts) on PO items (PO1–PO4).
\end{itemize}

Building on Section \ref{Lit:PO}, it was further proposed that PO prompts might affect broader user experience factors, which includes QoS, DT, sentiment of the SAV responses (Polarity and Subjectivity), PE, and BI:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{H6.1}: SAV2 (PO prompts) > SAV1 (Baseline) on these user experience metrics.

    \item \textbf{H6.2}: SAV4 (both PO and A prompts) > SAV3 (A prompts) on these user experience metrics.
\end{itemize}

\bigskip

\textbf{\textit{Anthropomorphism prompt effect}}

It was expected that incorporating anthropomorphic prompts in the SAV interface could enhance users’ perception of the vehicle’s anthropomorphic qualities. It was hypothesized that:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{H7.1}: SAV3 (A prompts) > SAV1 (Baseline) on A items (A1–A5).

    \item \textbf{H7.2}: SAV4 (both PO and A prompts) > SAV2 (PO prompts) on A items (A1–A5).
\end{itemize}

Similarly, building on Section \ref{Lit: A}, it was hypothesized that A prompts would also enhance these user experience dimensions:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{H8.1}: SAV3 (A prompts) > SAV1 (Baseline) on user experience metrics.

    \item \textbf{H8.2}: SAV4 (both PO and A prompts) > SAV2 (PO-only prompts) on user experience metrics.
\end{itemize}

\bigskip

\textbf{\textit{Combined prompt effect}}

Finally, an exploratory analysis examined the combined effects of PO and A prompts by comparing SAV4 (both PO and A prompts) against SAV1 (Baseline). It was expected to see that SAV4 outperforms SAV1 in all PO, A, and user experience metrics aspects.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{H9.1}: SAV4 (both PO and A prompts) > SAV1 (Baseline) on PO perception.

    \item \textbf{H9.2}: SAV4 (both PO and A prompts) > SAV1 (Baseline) on A items (A1–A5).

    \item \textbf{H9.3}: SAV4 (both PO and A prompts) > SAV1 (Baseline) on user experience metrics.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Qualitative analysis}

From the collected open-ended responses and interview data, seven themes were identified. Under each theme, various codes were developed to represent the aspects that participants mentioned as influencing the development of their psychological ownership when interacting with the SAVs. The themes and codes are as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Communication Style}: Short and concise, Long and detailed, Interactive and engaging, Verbose and repetitive, Guidance and recommendations, Use of ``I'', Use of ``we'';

    \item \textbf{Communication Tone}: Professional, polite, and formal, Kind and warm, Neutral;

    \item \textbf{Anthropomorphism}: Human-like, AI-/GPT-like, Robot-like, Machine-like;

    \item \textbf{Performance}: Meeting user’s needs, Efficiency, Care and support, Accuracy, Intelligence and conversation memory, Perceived ease of use, Taking initial actions;

    \item \textbf{Psychological Ownership Routes}: Personalization, Familiarity, intimacy and privacy, Feeling of control, Time and number of interactions;

    \item \textbf{Legal Ownership}: Fact of shared car, Like a taxi driver/service;

    \item \textbf{Sentiment}: Objective.

\end{itemize}

The effects of each code on participants’ perceptions of psychological ownership were analyzed, noting whether each influence was positive or negative, and the frequency of occurrences was recorded. The descriptions and sample responses illustrating each code are provided in Table \ref{tab:prompt_code_summary}.


