\subsection{Datasets}

We use four different subsets from the Amazon Product Reviews Dataset \cite{mcauley2015image}, namely: "Beauty", "Sports and Outdoors" (\textit{Sports} for short), "Toys and Games" (\textit{Toys} for short), and "Books". These subsets differ not only in the number of active users and items but also in the number of interactions among them. Table~\ref{tab:data_stats} shows summary statistics for each of these sets. For all datasets, we define a complementary item as an item bought together or after the query item. The datasets are filtered using a $n$-core procedure, i.e., only users and items with at least $n$ interactions are kept in the dataset. For sequential recommendation, we use a 5-core setup, and for graph-based recommendations, we use a 10-core setup as reported in \cite{rajput2024recommender} and \cite{wang2019neural}, respectively.


