%\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{L}{arge} language models (LLMs), with their massive parameter counts and rich training data, have demonstrated remarkable emergent capabilities~\cite{hoffmann2022training}. These capabilities span a wide range of applications, including open-domain question answering, code generation, commonsense reasoning, and even robotic control~\cite{chowdhery2023palm,thirunavukarasu2023large,demszky2023using,zhao2023survey,yang2024harnessing}. To efficiently integrate LLMs into wireless edge environments, the Hybrid Language Model (HLM) framework~\cite{hao2024hybrid} has emerged. HLM splits the inference task between the on-device small language model (SLM) and a remote LLM. Specifically, given a token—a basic unit of text generation such as a word or subword fragment—and the corresponding vocabulary $\mathcal{V}$, which denotes the full set of possible tokens the model can output, the SLM proposes a draft token based on given prompt tokens, which is then transmitted to the server. The server-side LLM then decides whether to accept the token or resample it. This speculative decoding approach~\cite{leviathan2023fast} ensures that the output distribution matches that of the LLM, preserving inference accuracy while exploiting distributed computation.

Despite its benefits, HLM suffers from severely limited token throughput due to its architectural overhead. Specifically, under our setup described in Section~\ref{sec:eval}, each token generation requires:
(i) uplink transmission of the full 32{,}000-token vocabulary distribution, incurring up to 92\,kB of payload per token; and
(ii) execution of both SLM and LLM inference, which take 25.6\,ms and 104.6\,ms, respectively—limiting the overall token throughput to fewer than 5 tokens per second.

To address this limitation, this work develops a communication-efficient HLM framework that significantly improves token throughput without compromising inference accuracy. The key insight is that if the on-device SLM can identify tokens likely to be accepted by the LLM, then both uplink communication and remote computation can be skipped. To enable this, we leverage \textit{uncertainty}, a measure of model confidence in its output. Empirical analysis reveals two key properties: (i) a strong correlation between the uncertainty of SLM-generated tokens and their rejection probability by the LLM, and (ii) a long-tail distribution in the SLM’s vocabulary probabilities.

Building on these insights, we first propose \textbf{U-HLM}, an uncertainty-aware HLM that opportunistically skips uplink communication and LLM computation for low-uncertainty tokens using a theoretically derived threshold. We then extend this to \textbf{Communication-efficient U-HLM (CU-HLM)}, which reduces uplink payloads by transmitting only the top-ranked vocabulary tokens for high-uncertainty cases.

To balance the trade-off between inference accuracy and communication efficiency, we analytically design both the uncertainty threshold and the compressed vocabulary size. Since inference accuracy is not directly tractable, we identify two primary sources of degradation: 
(i) erroneous skipping of tokens that should have been rejected (false positives), and 
(ii) inaccurate LLM resampling due to distortion in the compressed vocabulary distribution. 
We theoretically characterize both sources and optimize the threshold and compression level to keep their impact within acceptable bounds.

Furthermore, CU-HLM requires access to the full LLM-side vocabulary distribution to determine the optimal compression level, making it suitable only for statistical settings based on long-term observation. To enable fully on-device deployment, we propose \textbf{CU-HLM (Online)}, an online variant that dynamically adjusts the compressed vocabulary size based solely on token-level uncertainty—without any server-side feedback.

Through comprehensive experiments, we demonstrate that CU-HLM achieves up to \textbf{97.62\%} of HLM accuracy and even \textbf{101.85\%} of LLM inference accuracy, while substantially enhancing communication efficiency. Specifically, CU-HLM delivers up to \textbf{179.02\%} higher token throughput compared to LLM inference, with a favorable accuracy–throughput trade-off between its offline and online variants. Token throughput improvements over HLM are particularly pronounced under poor channel conditions.
These gains are driven by two key mechanisms:  
(i) uncertainty-aware opportunistic transmission, which limits uplink communication and LLM computation to just \textbf{25.2\%} of tokens—of which \textbf{85.7\%} are ultimately accepted; and  
(ii) vocabulary compression, which reduces uplink payloads by transmitting only a subset of tokens—averaging \textbf{2.6\%} of the vocabulary in the online variant and as little as \textbf{0.1\%} in the offline variant.
These results underscore CU-HLM as a practical and scalable solution for communication-efficient inference in bandwidth-constrained wireless edge environments.

\noindent \textbf{Related Works.} \quad 
LLMs such as Meta’s Llama series~\cite{touvron2023llama}, Google’s Gemma~\cite{team2024gemma}, and DeepSeek~\cite{guo2025deepseek} have demonstrated strong performance across a variety of tasks, including reasoning, information retrieval, and dialogue generation~\cite{yang2024harnessing}. However, their high computational complexity and memory demands make them impractical for on-device deployment~\cite{zhang2024fast, kandala2024tinyllm}. As a result, LLMs are typically hosted on remote servers, leading to considerable inference latency over wireless networks. This limitation has motivated the development of SLMs, such as TinyLlama~\cite{zhang2024tinyllama}, Phi-2~\cite{gunasekar2023textbooks}, and MobileVLM~\cite{chu2024mobilevlm}, as well as quantized LLMs~\cite{ray2024llmedge}, which enable lightweight inference that can be executed directly on mobile devices. To further enhance the efficiency and effectiveness of these models, numerous techniques such as model pruning, quantization, and knowledge distillation (KD) have been explored~\cite{ma2023llm, li2023loftq, zhou2023distillspec}. Nevertheless, these approaches often come at the cost of reduced inference accuracy~\cite{wang2024model, xu2023llmcad}, underscoring the trade-off between latency and accuracy in mobile AI deployment.

To address the trade-off between latency and accuracy in wireless edge inference, HLMs have emerged as a promising solution that leverages both device-side and server-side resources \cite{hao2024hybrid,she2025token,ray2024llmedge,xu2024edgellm}. In the HLM framework, a SLM is deployed on the device, and a LLM resides on a remote server. Inspired by speculative inference~\cite{leviathan2023fast, chen2023accelerating}, the HLM operates in two stages: the on-device SLM first generates a draft token in response to the user query, and the server-side LLM subsequently verifies whether the token should be accepted or resampled. This collaborative inference process ensures that the resulting output distribution is consistent with that of the LLM alone, thus preserving accuracy while distributing the computational load.

However, this architecture introduces considerable overhead. Specifically, for each token, the SLM must transmit its full vocabulary distribution to the server to enable verification or correction. In addition, both the SLM and LLM must perform inference for every token generation. This communication and dual computation requirement significantly limits token throughput. To address this limitation, some early works, such as~\cite{wang2023tabi}, propose query-level offloading, where the entire input is directed either to the on-device SLM or to the server-side LLM based on the estimated difficulty of the query. While simple and effective at a coarse level, such approaches lack the granularity needed for token-level control. Other methods aim to improve computational efficiency by applying model compression techniques such as pruning, quantization, and knowledge distillation~\cite{yu2024edge, huang2024edgellm, shi2024inferflow, ray2024llmedge}. However, these efforts primarily focus on reducing computational costs and often ignore the communication burden.

More recent studies have explored the joint optimization of communication and computation by selectively determining which tokens should be sent to the LLM for verification. This is a challenging task due to the need for accurate, low-latency decision-making. For instance, \cite{hao2024hybrid} computes the acceptance probability of each token using the LLM, but this approach still requires full LLM computation for every token. Other works~\cite{she2025token, zheng2025citer} leverage auxiliary AI techniques—such as multi-layer perceptrons (MLPs) or reinforcement learning—to determine whether individual tokens should be forwarded to the server. While these methods offer flexible token-level control, they incur additional training overhead and require architectural modifications, potentially limiting their feasibility in real-time or resource-constrained edge deployments.

In this context, we aim to develop a communication-efficient HLM framework that improves token throughput without compromising inference accuracy. Our key idea is to enable token-level decisions on whether to upload a token to the LLM—without incurring additional computational overhead.

\noindent \textbf{Contributions.} \quad The main contributions of this work are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We empirically identify two key properties of hybrid language modeling: (i) a strong linear correlation between the SLM’s token-level uncertainty and the LLM’s rejection probability, and (ii) a long-tail distribution in the SLM’s vocabulary output. 
    \item Based on these insights, we propose \textbf{CU-HLM}, a novel HLM framework that opportunistically skips communication and LLM computation for low-uncertainty tokens, and transmits a truncated vocabulary for high-uncertainty tokens—thereby improving communication efficiency without compromising accuracy.
    \item To support fully on-device deployment, we further introduce \textbf{CU-HLM (Online)}, an online variant that dynamically adapts the compressed vocabulary size at runtime without requiring server-side feedback.
    \item We analytically derive the optimal uncertainty threshold and compressed vocabulary size that balance inference accuracy and communication cost.
    \item Extensive experiments across diverse wireless fading channels, SNR conditions, model configurations, and datasets validate the effectiveness of our approach. Notably, CU-HLM achieves up to \textbf{101.85\%} of LLM accuracy and \textbf{179.02\%} higher token throughput, while reducing uplink transmissions by \textbf{74.8\%} and vocabulary payloads by \textbf{97.4\%}.
\end{itemize}
\fi

