This example has been previously explored in \cite{Unsolvableloops}, where only a closed formula is derived as $x_1(n)+x_2(n)=2^n(x_1(0)+x_2(0)+2)-(-1)^n/2-3/2$. In particular, they do not find any of the above invariants. 
We calculate truncated invariant ideals $I_{d, \mathcal{L}}$ for $d=1,2,3,4$, considering a given initial value. This covers all polynomial invariants up to degree 4 for Example~\ref{ex3} using Algorithm~\ref{alg2}. 
\end{example}

