\subsection{Diffusion model experiments}
To demonstrate the versatility of our definition of feature DP, we apply it to the use case of training diffusion models for image generation. Specifically, given a private image, we consider its blurred version as the public feature. This form of blurring operation is commonly perceived to protect the privacy of images such as in face-blurring~\citep{yang2022study}. Thus, under our definition, training on the blurred version of an image satisfies $(0, 0)$-FDP with the blurring operation $\Psi$ as the feature map. Our goal is to train diffusion models (from scratch) that are capable of generating image samples that are of higher quality compared to the blurred images while satisfying rigorous FDP guarantees.

% \myparagraph{Dataset} We consider two image datasets: Animal Faces HQ (AFHQ; \citep{choi2020stargan}) and LSUN~\citep{yu2015lsun}. AFHQ is a dataset containing 15K image samples of animal faces categorized into dog, cat, and wildlife. LSUN is an image dataset containing scenes such as dining rooms, bedrooms and churches; we take the subset of 3M images of bedrooms for training and evaluation. We scale and center-crop images in these datasets to $64 \times 64$ resolution. To construct the public feature $\Psi(x)$ for a private image $x$, we use the Gaussian blur implementation from the \href{https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow}{Pillow} library with radius $2.5$ (corresponding to kernel size $5$); see Fig. \ref{fig:blur_example} for an example.

