\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
%Software testing is a systematic process used to evaluate and verify that a software product functions correctly, meets requirements, and maintains high quality. As a critical part of the software development life cycle, testing focuses on identifying defects early, reducing the risk of failure when the software is in use \cite{garousi2016systematic, garousi2020exploring, neves2024elevating}. This process involves various approaches, such as manual and automated testing, and strategies like white-box, black-box, and grey-box testing, along with different testing types, each focused on specific aspects of the software. These types include unit, integration, system, acceptance, performance, usability, accessibility, smoke, security, load, and stress testing, among others \cite{mustafa2009classification, jamil2016software, alenzi2022survey}.

Software testing has changed significantly over the past decades, always evolving to support new challenges and technological advancements \cite{gillenson2018literature, garousi2016systematic}. Initially, testing was indistinguishable from debugging, relying on ad hoc methods to fix defects. By the 1950s-1970s, testing became a distinct practice focused on verifying correctness while debugging addressed failures. The 1970s-1980s saw a shift toward error detection, followed by the 1980s-1990s, when formal standards emphasized early defect prevention. In the post-1990s era, methodologies like Test-Driven Development embedded testing into the software lifecycle \cite{gillenson2018literature, gelperin1988growth}. Today, with the rise of machine learning and AI, testing has further evolved to address the non-deterministic nature of AI systems \cite{gao2019ai, tao2019testing}. %Unlike traditional software, AI models continuously adapt to data, requiring advanced quality assurance techniques. 

In the current landscape of software engineering, fairness testing has emerged as a crucial component of quality assurance, addressing the ethical challenges posed by AI-driven systems. These systems, capable of performing complex tasks such as recommendation, object detection, classification, and prediction, require advanced validation techniques to ensure their reliability and fairness \cite{brun2018software, verma2018fairness}. Unlike traditional software, AI models continuously adapt to data, making them susceptible to biases introduced through training data, algorithmic design, or societal inequalities \cite{desoftware}. Fairness testing, then, aims to systematically evaluate the ethical implications and potential biases embedded within AI systems, ensuring that decision-making processes lead to equitable outcomes across diverse demographic groups. By identifying and mitigating discriminatory risks based on factors such as race, gender, or socioeconomic status, fairness testing plays a key role in ensuring ethical AI development and promoting fairness in automated decision-making \cite{galhotra2017fairness, aggarwal2019black}. 

%Since Alan Turing's work in 1949, software testing has undergone significant evolution. In the \textit{debugging-oriented period} (pre-1950s), testing was merged with debugging, focusing on fixing defects using ad hoc methods. The \textit{demonstration-oriented period} (1950s-1970s) separated testing from debugging, with testing ensuring programs met requirements while debugging fixed crashes. By the \textit{destruction-oriented period} (1970s-1980s), testing aimed to expose errors, with debugging focusing on resolving them. In the \textit{evaluation and prevention-oriented period} (1980s-1990s), standards like the IEEE guidelines formalized testing to identify issues early. The \textit{test-driven-oriented period} (post-1990s) integrated testing into development through iterative methods like Scrum and Extreme Programming (XP), embedding testing within the coding process \cite{gillenson2018literature, gelperin1988growth}.

