\subsection{Periodic Heterogeneous three-dimensional sample}
A synthetic tissue sample composed of capillaries and cells is constructed in Fig.\ref{fig:tissue}a, where curve cylinders represent the capillaries and the cells are seen as spheres, where the diameter of the cells is \SI{6.25}{\micro\meter}. With a single wave with a wavelength of \SI{625} {\nano \meter} incident on the sample, Figure \ref{fig:tissue}b shows the intensity collected from the lower $z$ boundary of the sample. Another key advantage of the CW-based model is its periodicity. By efficiently calculating the wave behavior in a single unit cell and then applying it to the entire pattern, the simulation can be just as fast as for a single object. With the incident wavelength of $\lambda=$\SI{313}{\nano \meter}, the pairs c/d in Fig.\ref{fig:tissue} highlight the versatility of our model in handling simulations that require the representation of periodic elements.

